<body>
<div class="ltx_page_main">
<div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>This paper was partially supported by a fellowship from the
Japan Society for the Promotion of Science.</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Brian Osserman
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop a new, more functorial construction for the basic theory
of limit linear series, which provides a compactification of the
Eisenbud-Harris theory, and shows promise for generalization to
higher-dimensional varieties and higher-rank vector bundles.
We also give a result on lifting linear series from characteristic <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>
to characteristic <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. In an appendix, in order to obtain the necessary
dimensional lower bounds on our limit linear series scheme we develop a
theory of “linked Grassmannians;” these are schemes parametrizing
sub-bundles of a sequence of vector bundles which map into one another under
fixed maps of the ambient bundles.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Eisenbud-Harris theory of limit linear series of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> is a
powerful tool for degeneration arguments on curves, with applications to the
Kodaira dimension of moduli spaces of curves, and analysis of Weierstrass
points on curves, as well as new arguments for results such as
the Gieseker-Petri theorem. In this paper, we give a new construction for
limit linear series, very much in the spirit of Eisenbud and Harris’ theory,
but more functorial in nature, and involving a substantially new approach
which appears better suited to generalization to higher-dimensional
varieties and higher-rank vector bundles. The application of the theory of
limit linear series in positive characteristic is fundamental to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>;
we should remark that we do not see any obstructions to the original
construction of Eisenbud and Harris working in
characteristic <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, but the independence of characteristic is more
transparent in the functorial setting. We begin with an overview of the
basic ideas of limit linear series; for those unfamiliar with linear series,
the actual definitions and notation are all recalled below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While our main theorem (see Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 5.3. ‣ 5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>) is too technical to
state in an introduction, we can
outline the main concepts involved. The basic idea of limit linear series is
to analyze how linear series behave as a family of smooth curves <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/B</annotation></semantics></math>
degenerates to a nodal curve <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>; a key distinguishing feature of the
theory is that rather than standard deformation-theoretic techniques to
obtain results from the degeneration, a simple dimension count on the
special fiber produces results immediately.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">More specifically, recall
that a proper, geometrically reduced and connected
nodal curve with smooth components is said to be of <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">compact type</span> if
the dual graph is a tree, or equivalently if the (connected component of
the) Picard scheme is proper.
Now, if <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is not of compact type, line bundles on the smooth curves
may not limit to a line bundle on the nodal fiber, as the Picard scheme of
the family (and specifically of the nodal fiber) will not be proper. On the
other hand, if the nodal fiber is reducible, limiting line bundles will
exist, but will
not be unique, as one can always twist by one of the components of the
reducible fiber to get a new line bundle, isomorphic away from the nodal
fiber to the original one. However, this turns out to be the only ambiguity.
To explain the approach to this issue, we consider for simplicity the case
of <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔤</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>’s where the family <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/B</annotation></semantics></math> has smooth general fiber, and <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
consists of two smooth components <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, meeting at a single node <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a line bundle of degree <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, we will say it has degree
<math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,d-i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,d-i)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> if it restricts to a line bundle of degree <math id="S1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and
degree <math id="S1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-i</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.
Eisenbud and Harris approached the problem by considering the linear
series obtained by looking at the two possible limit line bundles obtained
by requiring degrees <math id="S1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,d)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the degree <math id="S1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>
components cannot contribute anything to the
space of global sections chosen for the <math id="S1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔤</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, this is equivalent to
specifying a <math id="S1.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔤</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> on each of <math id="S1.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>; they showed that
if such a pair arises as a limit of <math id="S1.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔤</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>’s from the smooth fibers, it will
satisfy the ramification condition</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}^{Y}(P)+a_{r-i}^{Z}(P)\geq d," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}^{Y}(P)+a_{r-i}^{Z}(P)\geq d,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S1.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{i}^{Y}(P)\}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{i}^{Y}(P)\}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{i}^{Z}(P)\}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{i}^{Z}(P)\}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the vanishing
sequences on <math id="S1.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> at the node. Eisenbud and Harris refer to such
pairs on a nodal
fiber as <span id="S1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">crude limit series</span>, and when the inequality is replaced by
an equality, as <span id="S1.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">refined limit series</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Eisenbud and Harris’ moduli scheme construction requires restriction to
refined limit series, and as such is not generally proper, and is also
necessarily disconnected, being constructed as a disjoint union over the
different possible ramification indices at the nodes. Moreover, the
necessity to specify ramification indices makes it unsuitable for
generalizing from curves to higher-dimensional varieties. The basic idea of
our construction is to remember not just the line bundles of degree <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,0)</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,d)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, but also the <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1</annotation></semantics></math> line bundles of degree <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,d-i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,d-i)</annotation></semantics></math>
that lie in between. One can then replace the ramification condition with
a simpler compatibility condition on the corresponding spaces
of global sections, yielding a very functorial approach to constructing the
moduli scheme. Further, one can show a high degree of compatibility with
Eisenbud and Harris’ construction: in particular, for a curve (of compact
type) over a field, our construction contains the Eisenbud-Harris version as
an open subscheme.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We begin in Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Linear Series in Arbitrary Characteristic ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> with a review of the basics
of linear series, but in arbitrary characteristic. In Section
<a href="#S3" title="3. Smoothing Families ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we give the precise conditions on the families of
curves we will consider, and show that such families may be contructed as
necessary. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4. The Relative 
        
          
            
            
              
              G
              r
            
            d
          
        
       Functor ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we define the limit linear series
functors we will consider, and our main theorem, the representability of
these functors, is proved in Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>; we conclude with
corollaries as in Eisenbud and Harris on smoothing linear series from the
special fiber when the dimension is as expected, including in the cases of
positive and mixed characteristic. We compare our
theory to that of Eisenbud and Harris in Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, and
conclude with some further questions in Section <a href="#S7" title="7. Further Questions ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
Finally, in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> we develop a theory of linked
Grassmannian schemes, which parametrize collections of sub-bundles of a
sequence of vector bundles linked together by maps between the bundles; this
is used in the construction of the limit linear series scheme in the main
theorem, and in particular to obtain the necessary lower bound on its
dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The work here is of course entirely inspired by Eisenbud and Harris’ original
construction in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Attempts to generalize this theory have thus
far been sparse, but include, for instance, work of Esteves <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> and
of Teixidor i Bigas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> to generalize to certain curves not of
compact type and higher-rank vector bundles respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The contents of this paper form a portion of the author’s 2004 PhD thesis at
MIT, under the direction of Johan de Jong.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgements</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">I would like to thank Johan de Jong for his tireless and invaluable
guidance. I would also like to thank Jason Starr, Joe Harris, Max
Lieblich, and Steve Kleiman for their many helpful discussions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Linear Series in Arbitrary Characteristic</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before getting into the technical definitions related to the central
construction, we begin with a few preliminary definitions and lemmas in the
case of a smooth, proper, geometrically integral curve <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>,
over a field <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of any characteristic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, recall:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle of degree <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> an
<math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspace of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(C,{\mathscr{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(C,{\mathscr{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>, we call the pair <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> a
a <span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">linear series</span> of degree <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and dimension <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, or a <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔤</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔤</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and a point <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a unique
sequence of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math> increasing integers <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{({\mathscr{L}},V)}_{i}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{({\mathscr{L}},V)}_{i}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> called the <span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">vanishing sequence</span> of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, given by the orders
of vanishing at <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> of sections in <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. We also define
<math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{({\mathscr{L}},V)}_{i}(P):=a^{({\mathscr{L}},V)}_{i}(P)-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{({\mathscr{L}},V)}_{i}(P):=a^{({\mathscr{L}},V)}_{i}(P)-i</annotation></semantics></math>, the <span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ramification sequence</span>
of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">unramified</span> at <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> if all
<math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{({\mathscr{L}},V)}_{i}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{({\mathscr{L}},V)}_{i}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> are zero; otherwise, it is <span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ramified</span> at <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_warn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Warning 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since the ramification and vanishing sequences are equivalent
data, we tend to refer to conditions stated in terms of either one simply as
“ramification conditions.” We will also drop the <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> superscript or
replace it as appropriate, particularly when we have a linear series on
each component of a reducible curve, when we will tend to simply use the
component to indicate which series we are referring to.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following definitions, being tailored to characteristic <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, may be less
standard:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say a linear series <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">separable</span> if it
is not
everywhere ramified. Otherwise, it is <span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">inseparable</span>. At a point <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>,
we say that <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">tamely ramified</span> if the characteristic is <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>
or if the vanishing orders <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> are maximally distributed mod
<math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> (in particular, this holds at any unramified point).
Otherwise, we say that <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">wildly ramified</span> at <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The following result is a characteristic-<math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> version of a standard Plücker
formula, whose proof simply adapts standard techniques:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth, proper, geometrically
integral curve of genus <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> over a field <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔤</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Then either <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> is inseparable, or we have the inequality</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{P\in C}\sum_{i}\alpha_{i}(P)\leq(r+1)d+\binom{r+1}{2}(2g-2)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{P\in C}\sum_{i}\alpha_{i}(P)\leq(r+1)d+\binom{r+1}{2}(2g-2).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, this will be an equality if and only if <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> is everywhere
tamely ramified; in particular, in this case inseparability is impossible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We simply use the argument of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Prop. 1.1]</cite>.
Even though it is intended for characteristic <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, the proof follows through
equally well in characteristic <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> for our modified statement, noting that
their “Taylor expansion” map is defined independent of characteristic, and
their formulas then hold on a formal level. Indeed, their
argument shows that if <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> induces a non-zero section
<math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{\otimes r+1}\otimes(\Omega^{1}_{C})^{\otimes\binom{r+1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{\otimes r+1}\otimes(\Omega^{1}_{C})^{\otimes\binom{r+1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get the desired
inequality, with equality if and only if the determinant of their Lemma 1.2
is non-zero at all <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> (where, as in the proof of the proposition, <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}:=\alpha^{({\mathscr{L}},V)}_{j}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}:=\alpha^{({\mathscr{L}},V)}_{j}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>). In fact, if this determinant is non-zero anywhere,
we see also that <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> has finite order of vanishing at that point, and
cannot be the zero section. Next, their same lemma shows that their
determinant will be non-zero at a point <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> is tamely
ramified at <math id="S2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that if we show that inseparability corresponds
precisely to having <math id="S2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s({\mathscr{L}},V)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s({\mathscr{L}},V)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we are done. But
this also follows trivially, since on the one hand any unramified point is in
particular tamely ramified, and will in fact give a non-vanishing point of
<math id="S2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math>, and on the other hand, if <math id="S2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s({\mathscr{L}},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s({\mathscr{L}},V)</annotation></semantics></math> is non-zero, we have
seen that we can get only finitely many ramification points.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Note that because vanishing sequences are bounded by <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, if
<math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&lt;p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&lt;p</annotation></semantics></math>, then wild ramification is not possible, so the previous proposition
immediately implies:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Wildly ramified or inseparable linear series of degree <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> are
only possible when <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we have the notation:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>
ramification sequences <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{i}=\{\alpha^{i}_{j}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{i}=\{\alpha^{i}_{j}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we write <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:=\rho(g,r,d;\alpha^{i}):=(r+1)(d-r)-rg-\sum_{i,j}\alpha^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:=\rho(g,r,d;\alpha^{i}):=(r+1)(d-r)-rg-\sum_{i,j}\alpha^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is the <span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">expected dimension</span> of linear series of degree <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and
dimension <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> on a curve of genus <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, with at least the specified
ramification at the <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Smoothing Families</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we describe the families of curves whose limit linear
series we will study, called “smoothing families”, and then give
some basic existence results. While the definition of a smoothing family is
rather technical, we expect that most applications will involve smoothing a
given reducible curve over a one-dimensional base, so we conclude with a
theorem giving the existence of such families satisfying all our technical
conditions, given the desired reducible fiber. However, we work over a fairly
arbitrary base, because this allows the use of arguments in the universal
setting, in negative expected dimension, and in certain pathological cases
where expected dimension is satisfied, but only over a positive-dimensional
“special fiber”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our central technical definition is:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A morphism of schemes <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math>, together with
sections <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\dots,P_{n}:B\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\dots,P_{n}:B\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> constitutes a <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">smoothing family</span> if:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(I)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is regular and connected;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(II)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is flat and proper;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(III)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The fibers of <math id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> are genus-<math id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curves of compact type;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(IV)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The images of the <math id="S3.I1.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint and contained in the smooth
locus of <math id="S3.I1.ix4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(V)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each connected component <math id="S3.I1.ix5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of the singular locus of <math id="S3.I1.ix5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> maps
isomorphically onto its scheme-theoretic image <math id="S3.I1.ix5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.I1.ix5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, and
furthermore <math id="S3.I1.ix5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X|_{\pi^{-1}\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X|_{\pi^{-1}\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> breaks into two (not necessarily
irreducible) components intersecting along <math id="S3.I1.ix5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(VI)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Any point in the singular locus of <math id="S3.I1.ix6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> which is smoothed
in the generic fiber is regular in the total space of <math id="S3.I1.ix6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(VII)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exist sections <math id="S3.I1.ix7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contained in the smooth locus of
<math id="S3.I1.ix7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> such that every irreducible component of any geometric fiber of <math id="S3.I1.ix7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>
meets at least one of the <math id="S3.I1.ix7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with a lemma on two methods of obtaining new smoothing families
from a given one:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/B</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be a smoothing family. Then</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}\rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}\rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is either a </span><math id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-valued point of </span><math id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for any
field </span><math id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, a localization of </span><math id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, or a smooth morphism with </span><math id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> connected,
then base change to </span><math id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> gives a new smoothing family.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a node of
</span><math id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/B</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> which is not smoothed in the generic fiber, let </span><math id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y,Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y,Z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be the components
of </span><math id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\cup Z=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\cup Z=X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\cap Z=\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\cap Z=\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then restriction to </span><math id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or
</span><math id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> gives a new smoothing family.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For (i), the only properties of a smoothing family not preserved under arbitrary base change are (I) and (VI), which are easily checked in our specific cases.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">For (ii), the only condition that isn’t immediately clear is that
flatness is preserved. However, this follows from the exact sequence of sheaves on <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{X}\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{Y}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{Z}\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{Y\cap Z}\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{X}\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{Y}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{Z}\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{Y\cap Z}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">together with the hypothesized flatness of <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{Y\cap Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{Y\cap Z}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We now proceed to develop some results on construction of smoothing
families.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math> be a family
satisfying conditions
(I)-(III) and (VI) of a smoothing family, <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{X}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{X}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> a chosen geometric
fiber of <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> mapping to a point <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in B</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{P}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{P}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> smooth closed points on <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{X}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{X}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with images in <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> having
residue fields separable
extensions of <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(P)</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose further that each component <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of
the singular locus of <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/B</annotation></semantics></math> is flat over its image <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there is an
étale base change of <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and sections <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> specializing to the
<math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{P}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{P}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> which yield a smoothing family still containing <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{X}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{X}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as a
geometric fiber, and with
the same geometric generic fiber as <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We can Zariski localize <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> to avoid any components of the
singular locus not occurring in <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and to insure that the sections we will contruct are disjoint and in the smooth locus. First, in addition to the <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{P}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{P}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, choose one
smooth closed point <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{D}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{D}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> on each component of <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, each having
field of definition a separable extension of <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(P)</annotation></semantics></math> (this is possible
by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Prop. 2.2.16 and Cor. 2.2.13]</cite>). Next, by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Prop. 2.2.14]</cite>, after possible étale base change we can
find the desired sections <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, each going through the
corresponding <math id="S3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{P}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{P}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{D}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{D}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. All that remains is to show that
we can obtain condition (V) as well. Since the singular locus of a family of
nodal curves is finite and unramified over the base, our flatness hypothesis
implies that each connected component <math id="S3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is étale over its image
<math id="S3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and using <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Prop. 2.3.8 b)]</cite> together with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Cor.
V.1, p. 52]</cite> in the case that <math id="S3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\neq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\neq B</annotation></semantics></math>, after an étale base change <math id="S3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> will map isomorphically to <math id="S3.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, giving the first half of (V).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we need to make sure that <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> breaks
into components around each node. For each connected component <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of
the singular locus of <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>,
it suffices to produce an étale base change which causes the generic fiber
<math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}^{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}^{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X|_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X|_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>
to break. By hypothesis, <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}^{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}^{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> breaks geometrically, and it will
break into components over an intermediate field <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
the geometric components are <math id="S3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}(\bar{K}/K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}(\bar{K}/K)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant. But this may be accompished after étale base change by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Lem. 4.2]</cite>, again using <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Cor. V.1, p. 52]</cite> in the case <math id="S3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\neq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\neq B</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For typical applications of limit linear series, we expect that the
following theorem, which follows fairly easily from a theorem of Winters,
will render irrelevant the technical hypotheses of our smoothing families:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be any curve of compact type over an algebraically
closed field <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{P}_{1},\dots,\bar{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{P}_{1},\dots,\bar{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> distinct smooth closed
points.
Then <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> may be placed into a smoothing family <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/B</annotation></semantics></math> with sections <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
specializing to the <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{P}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{P}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is a curve over <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and where
the generic fiber of <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Setting all <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
we can apply <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, Prop. 4.2]</cite> to obtain a proper map over
<math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}k</annotation></semantics></math> from some regular surface <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to some regular curve
<math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, having <math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as a fiber. This must automatically be flat, and if we localize <math id="S3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{B}</annotation></semantics></math> we can assume all fibers are at most nodal. We then claim that the
generic fiber <math id="S3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> must be smooth: indeed, all the local rings are regular by hypothesis, so by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Cor. 16.21]</cite> the residue fields of any non-smooth points would have to be inseparable over <math id="S3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(B)</annotation></semantics></math>, which cannot happen in the case of nodes, since they are always unramified. Since the base field is algebraically closed, we need not worry about separability
of residue field extensions on the closed fiber. Finally, our nodes are
all isolated points, so the map to their image is a finite, unramified map of local schemes with algebraically closed residue field, and hence an isomorphism. Therefore, we can
apply the preceding lemma to obtain our desired smoothing family.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are a number of differences between our definition of a
smoothing family, and the one used in Eisenbud and Harris’ original
construction in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. None of these are due to the different
construction. Extra conditions such as the reducedness of <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and the
regularity of <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> at smoothed nodes are in fact necessary to ensure that
certain closed subschemes are actually Cartier divisors, and the condition
that <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> break into
distinct components above the nodes is likewise tacitly assumed, but not
automatic. The regularity of <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is necessary to make the sort of
dimension-count arguments employed in the construction. Conversely, the
hypotheses on the characteristic (or even existence) of a base field appears
to be unnecessary in their construction, as does the hypothesis that the
relatively ample divisor be disjoint from the ramification sections. The
only hypothesis we include here that may be truly gratuitous is that the
relatively ample divisor be composed of global sections, but it is
convenient and, as we have shown, not difficult to achieve.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We do not claim that the moduli scheme could not be constructed
under weaker hypotheses, but merely that our hypotheses are those which
are necessary for our particular argument. It seems quite likely that one
could drop many of the hypotheses on both <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> if one carried out the construction in a universal setting and then pulled back the result to arbitrary families.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is not true that condition (VI) of a
smoothing family is
preserved under base change by arbitrary closed immersions <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}\rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}\rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math>, even when <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is regular and connected. For example, consider any
smoothing family with <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B={\mathbb{A}}^{2}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B={\mathbb{A}}^{2}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and having a node <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> given by the <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-axis. Then if <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the parabola <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=x^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=x^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, base
change to <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> will create a singularity in <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> above the origin.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, the hypotheses for a smoothing family <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> imply that
every connected component of the singular locus of <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is regular, and in
particular irreducible and reduced. However, we will not need this, so we do not pursue it.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>The Relative <math id="S4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> Functor</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given, in addition to a smoothing family, integers <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,d</annotation></semantics></math>, and ramification
sequences <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{i}:=\{\alpha_{j}^{i}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{i}:=\{\alpha_{j}^{i}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for each of
our <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we will associate a <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> functor to our smoothing family;
this
functor will initially appear to include a lot of extraneous data, but we
will show that it actually gives the “right” functor, at least in the sense
that it associates a reasonable set to any geometric point of <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, before defining the functor, we give some preliminary lemmas and definitions. In order to
ensure that our functor is globally well-defined, we will need the following easily-verified lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper morphism with
geometrically reduced and
connected fibers, <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> two isomorphic line bundles on <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> sub-modules of <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Then the
property that “<math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> maps into
<math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>” is independent of the choice of isomorphism between <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also describe a generalized notion of sub-bundle:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math> be a morphism of schemes, and <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> a line bundle on <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. A sub-sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to
be a <span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">sub-bundle</span> of <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> if in addition to <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> being a locally
free sheaf, for any <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math>, the map
<math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V_{S}\rightarrow\pi_{S*}{\mathscr{L}}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{S}\rightarrow\pi_{S*}{\mathscr{L}}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> remains injective.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that in this definition, we are pushing forward the pullback of <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and not the other way around. The required sheaf map is gotten by composing
the induced map <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{S}\rightarrow(\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}})_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{S}\rightarrow(\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}})_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> with the natural map <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}})_{S}\rightarrow\pi_{S*}{\mathscr{L}}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}})_{S}\rightarrow\pi_{S*}{\mathscr{L}}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we define ramification conditions in this context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/B</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper relative curve with line bundle <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a sub-bundle of <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/B</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> a smooth section of <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the sequence of maps</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}|_{(d+1)P}\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}|_{dP}\rightarrow\dots,\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}|_{P}\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}|_{(d+1)P}\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}|_{dP}\rightarrow\dots,\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}|_{P}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We denote by <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> the composition map <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}|_{mP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}|_{mP}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a sequence of <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> increasing integers <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>,
we say that <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> has <span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">vanishing sequence at least <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{j}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{j}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, or <span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ramification sequence at least <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{j}-j\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{j}-j\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, if <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}\beta_{m}\leq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}\beta_{m}\leq j</annotation></semantics></math> for
all <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq a^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq a^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, to simplify notation, and because it will be enough for inductive degenerations, we will restrict our families to reducible
curves with only two components.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_sit">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Situation 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assume that <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/B</annotation></semantics></math> is a smoothing family with
at most one node (in the sense that
the singular locus of <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible). If there is a node, we
introduce some notation: denote by <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> the singular locus of <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> its image in <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>; by hypothesis, <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
isomorphically to <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. We now distinguish three cases: case (1) is that
there is no node; case (2) is that <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is all of <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>; and case (3) is
that <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is a Cartier divisor on <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. In cases (2) and (3), we denote by <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> the components of <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X|_{\pi^{-1}\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X|_{\pi^{-1}\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>, necessarily smooth and intersecting along <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that with the specified hypotheses, these three cases are all the
possibilities: indeed, since <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is regular, completing and examining the universal deformation of a nodal curve described <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, p. 82]</cite> easily shows that if <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is non-empty, it is
locally generated principally. Note that with no hypotheses on the base, this would not be true; in fact, one can construct a families of nodal curves over a quadric cone having a node whose image is the union of three lines through the cone point.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In case (2), we will make use
of the natural morphism (actually an isomorphism onto a connected component)
<math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}^{d-i}(Y_{T}/T)\times\operatorname{Pic}^{i}(Z_{T}/T)\rightarrow\operatorname{Pic}^{d}(X_{T}/T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}^{d-i}(Y_{T}/T)\times\operatorname{Pic}^{i}(Z_{T}/T)\rightarrow\operatorname{Pic}^{d}(X_{T}/T)</annotation></semantics></math> for any
<math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, in order to think of a pair of line bundles <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}_{Y},{\mathscr{L}}_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}_{Y},{\mathscr{L}}_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> as a line bundle on <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we will denote <math id="S4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}}_{Y},{\mathscr{L}}_{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}}_{Y},{\mathscr{L}}_{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that this is only defined up agreement locally on the base, but this
will not be a problem as we will consider the sheafified Picard functor.
In case (3), by the nonsingularity
hypothesis, <math id="S4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> are Cartier divisors in <math id="S4.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, so we have associated
line bundles on <math id="S4.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, because <math id="S4.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is a
Cartier divisor on <math id="S4.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y+Z)\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(\pi^{*}\Delta)\cong\pi^{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{B}(\Delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y+Z)\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(\pi^{*}\Delta)\cong\pi^{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{B}(\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that locally on <math id="S4.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y+Z)\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y+Z)\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Given a morphism <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:T\rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:T\rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by a subscript <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> the various
pullbacks under <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. We now describe our functor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The functor <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}(X/B,\{(P_{i},\alpha^{i})\}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}(X/B,\{(P_{i},\alpha^{i})\}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> associates to <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T/B</annotation></semantics></math> the set of objects described as follows, modulo the equivalence induced by sheafification with respect to Zariski localization of <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">Case (1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a line bundle <math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, together with a
rank <math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> sub-bundle <math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>, having ramification sequence at
least <math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> along the <math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i,T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">Case (2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a line bundle <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, which has degree
<math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> when restricted to <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and degree <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, together with rank
<math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> sub-bundles <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0},\dots,V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0},\dots,V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{i}:=({\mathscr{L}}|_{Y_{T}}(-i\Delta^{\prime}_{T}),{\mathscr{L}}|_{Z_{T}}(i\Delta^{\prime}_{T}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{i}:=({\mathscr{L}}|_{Y_{T}}(-i\Delta^{\prime}_{T}),{\mathscr{L}}|_{Z_{T}}(i\Delta^{\prime}_{T}))</annotation></semantics></math>. Each <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> must map
to <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> under the natural map given by inclusion on <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and each <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> must map to <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> under inclusion on <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, we impose ramification along the <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i,T}</annotation></semantics></math> as in case
(1), with the caveat that we impose it only on <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is on <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>,
and only on <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is on <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">Case (3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a line bundle <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, which has degree <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> when
restricted to <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and degree <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, together with rank <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> sub-bundles <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0},\dots,V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0},\dots,V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{T*}({\mathscr{L}}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T*}({\mathscr{L}}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{i}:=({\mathscr{L}}\otimes{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y)_{T}^{\otimes i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{i}:=({\mathscr{L}}\otimes{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y)_{T}^{\otimes i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Each <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> must map to <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> under the natural
map <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{T*}({\mathscr{L}}^{i})\rightarrow\pi_{T*}({\mathscr{L}}^{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T*}({\mathscr{L}}^{i})\rightarrow\pi_{T*}({\mathscr{L}}^{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Further, locally on <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y+Z)_{T}\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y+Z)_{T}\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we require that <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> map to
<math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> under the induced map. Finally, we impose
the desired ramification along the <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i,T}</annotation></semantics></math> as in the first two cases,
imposing it only on <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> specializes to <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, and only on <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
if <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> specializes to <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4. The Relative 
        
          
            
            
              
              G
              r
            
            d
          
        
       Functor ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, our functor is well-defined despite the fact that we cannot distinguish line bundles on <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> which are isomorphic locally on <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. By the same token, the compatibility condition
on <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in case (3) is independent of choice of local isomorphisms
<math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y+Z)\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y+Z)\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it isn’t hard to see that the definition of
<math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> in cases (2) and (3) is independent of the choice of <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We also have:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the subfunctor of <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of
those linear series which are separable in every fiber. This is
self-explanatory for smooth curves, while for reducible curves we require
both <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}|_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}|_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}|_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}|_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> to be separable.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">One can verify quite directly that the <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> we have defined is in
fact a functor. However, since we defined it differently in three separate
cases, we also need to check:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are
compatible
with base change.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose we pull back from <math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/B</annotation></semantics></math> to a new
smoothing family <math id="S4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}/B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}/B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The only case with anything to check is if case
(3) pulls back to case (1) or (2). For the former, we note that <math id="S4.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S4.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> pull back to the trivial bundle, so all the maps between the
<math id="S4.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphisms. For the latter, the point is that <math id="S4.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> clearly
pulls back to <math id="S4.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{Z^{\prime}}(\Delta^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{Z^{\prime}}(\Delta^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, from which one checks that locally
on the base, <math id="S4.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> pulls back to <math id="S4.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{Z^{\prime}}(-\Delta^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{Z^{\prime}}(-\Delta^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly with
<math id="S4.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> switched.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Lastly, because <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> was defined as a sub-functor of <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> in
terms of behavior on fibers, it immediately follows that it too is
compatible with base change.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Representability</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main theorem is the representability of our <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> functors, together with a lower bound on its dimension.
However, to ease the pain of the proof, we begin
with some technical lemmas before proceeding to the statement and proof of
the main theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with some compatibility checks on our notion of sub-bundle:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Our notion of sub-bundle has the following
desireable properties:</span></p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose we have </span><math id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is locally free, and the
higher derived pushforward functors vanish. Then our definition of
sub-bundle of </span><math id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is equivalent to the usual one (that is, a locally
free sub-sheaf with locally free quotient).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an effective Cartier divisor on </span><math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, flat over </span><math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
any line bundle on </span><math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then a sub-sheaf </span><math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a sub-bundle
of </span><math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if and only if it is a sub-bundle of </span><math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}(D)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> under the
natural inclusion.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be sub-bundles of rank </span><math id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in our
sense, and suppose </span><math id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}\subset V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}\subset V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then </span><math id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}=V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}=V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">For (i), we first note that by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, Cor. 6.9.9]</cite> (see
also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, 6.2.1]</cite>), the natural map
<math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}})_{S}\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}})_{S}\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is an
isomorphism. Now, if the quotient <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q:=\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}/V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q:=\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}/V</annotation></semantics></math> is locally
free, we have <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{S}\hookrightarrow(\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}})_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{S}\hookrightarrow(\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}})_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>,
and therefore <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{S}\hookrightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{S}\hookrightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, as desired.
Conversely, <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{S}\hookrightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{S}\hookrightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> means that the
injectivity
of <math id="S5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> is preserved under base change; this in turn
implies that <math id="S5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tor}^{1}_{S}({\mathscr{O}}_{S},Q_{S})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Tor</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tor}^{1}_{S}({\mathscr{O}}_{S},Q_{S})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> has
vanishing <math id="S5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Tor</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tor}</annotation></semantics></math>. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Prop. 6.1]</cite>, we conclude that <math id="S5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is flat,
hence locally free, completing the proof of (i).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assertion (ii) will follow immediately if show that
<math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}_{S}\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}(D)_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}_{S}\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}(D)_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective for all <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. However, by the flatness of <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, the cokernel of
<math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}\hookrightarrow{\mathscr{L}}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}\hookrightarrow{\mathscr{L}}(D)</annotation></semantics></math> is flat over <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, so injectivity of this map is preserved under base change, and applying <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> gives the desired result.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, (iii) is straightforward: let <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=V_{2}/V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=V_{2}/V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in B</annotation></semantics></math> be
any point of <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. If we base change to <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}\kappa(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}\kappa(b)</annotation></semantics></math>, we get find from the definition of sub-bundle that <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1b}\hookrightarrow V_{2b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1b}\hookrightarrow V_{2b}</annotation></semantics></math>, so since their dimension is the same, we get <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{b}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{b}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and by Nakayama’s lemma we conclude <math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}=V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}=V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, as asserted.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We also have a lemma illustrating how we will use our sections <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/B</annotation></semantics></math> be a smoothing family, and <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> any
finite collection of line bundles on <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
of degree <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists an effective divisor <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying:</span></p>
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is flat over </span><math id="S5.I2.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and supported in the smooth locus of </span><math id="S5.I2.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I2.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}(D)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is locally free and </span><math id="S5.I2.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}(D)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}(D)=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S5.I2.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I2.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}(D)\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}(D)|_{mP_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}(D)\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}(D)|_{mP_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is surjective for
all </span><math id="S5.I2.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and all </span><math id="S5.I2.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq d+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq d+1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iv)</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In case (3) of Situation </span><a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="Situation 4.4. ‣ 4. The Relative 
        
          
            
            
              
              G
              r
            
            d
          
        
       Functor ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a><span id="S5.I2.ix4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S5.I2.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.ix4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> may be written as </span><math id="S5.I2.ix4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{Y}+D^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{Y}+D^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.ix4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, with </span><math id="S5.I2.ix4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{Y}|_{\pi^{-1}\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{Y}|_{\pi^{-1}\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.ix4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> contained in </span><math id="S5.I2.ix4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.ix4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and similarly for </span><math id="S5.I2.ix4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.ix4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, étale locally on <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> we may require that <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is disjoint from the <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as well.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">With <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> any collection of sections as in the definition of a smoothing
family, let <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}=\sum_{i}D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}=\sum_{i}D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>; then <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>-ample, so locally on
<math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large, <math id="S5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> will have the desired properties. Since <math id="S5.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian, we can choose such an <math id="S5.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> globally.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">The étale-local disjointness assertion is obtained by constructing new sections étale locally as in the proof of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3. Smoothing Families ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, choosing the <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{D}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{D}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to be distinct from the <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{P}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{P}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We can now prove our central result:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\dots,P_{n}:B\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\dots,P_{n}:B\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> is a
smoothing family satisfying the two-component hypothesis of Situation
<a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="Situation 4.4. ‣ 4. The Relative 
        
          
            
            
              
              G
              r
            
            d
          
        
       Functor ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, and <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{i}:=\{\alpha^{i}_{j}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{i}:=\{\alpha^{i}_{j}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> ramification sequences,
then <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}={\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}(X/B;\{(P_{i},\alpha^{i})\}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}={\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}(X/B;\{(P_{i},\alpha^{i})\}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a scheme <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, compatible with
base change to any other smoothing family.
This scheme is projective, and if it is non-empty, the local ring at
any point <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> closed in its fiber over <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in B</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension at
least <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim{\mathscr{O}}_{B,b}+\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim{\mathscr{O}}_{B,b}+\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\rho(g,r,d;\alpha^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\rho(g,r,d;\alpha^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> as in
Definition <a href="#S2.Thmthm6" title="Definition 2.6. ‣ 2. Linear Series in Arbitrary Characteristic ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>. Furthermore, <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is also
representable, and is naturally an open subscheme of <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Once the <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> functor has been defined, the proof of its
representability is long but for the most part extremely straightforward,
using nothing more than the well-known representability of the various
functors in terms of which we have described <math id="S5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. The one trick, borrowed from Eisenbud and Harris, is to twist a universal line bundle
<math id="S5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> by a high power of an ample divisor so sub-bundles of its pushforward are parametrized by a standard Grassmannian
scheme. The dimension count is an altogether different story; it is
harder than in Eisenbud and Harris’ construction, and is essentially the
subject of the final section this chapter.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">We note that our functor is visibly a Zariski sheaf, so we can check representability Zariski locally on <math id="S5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, it is clear that imposing ramification conditions, consisting of imposing rank conditions on sequences of maps of locally free sheaves, give closed subfunctors, so it suffices to check representability without imposing ramification.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">As in defining the functor, we have three cases to consider. The first is
the simplest. We start in this case with the relative Picard scheme
<math id="S5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\operatorname{Pic}^{d}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\operatorname{Pic}^{d}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math>, obtained for instance from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Thm. 9.4.1]</cite> and
twisting by sections to obtain the desired degree. We denote by <math id="S5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
the universal line bundle on <math id="S5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{B}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{B}P</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be the divisor provided by Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 5.2. ‣ 5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> for <math id="S5.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
viewing <math id="S5.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{B}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{B}P</annotation></semantics></math> as a smoothing family over <math id="S5.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, we let <math id="S5.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be
the relative Grassmannian scheme of <math id="S5.p12.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{P*}(\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}(D))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{P*}(\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}(D))</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define our <math id="S5.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> scheme to be the closed subscheme
of <math id="S5.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> cut out by the condition that any sub-bundle <math id="S5.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S5.p12.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{P*}(\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}(D))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{P*}(\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}(D))</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes on <math id="S5.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, which naturally gives a closed subscheme cut out locally by
minors. This completes the construction in the first case.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, in the second case, we use the Picard schemes <math id="S5.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{i}:=\operatorname{Pic}^{d-i,i}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{i}:=\operatorname{Pic}^{d-i,i}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math>, the schemes parametrizing line
bundles on <math id="S5.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with degrees <math id="S5.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> when restricted to <math id="S5.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>
respectively. These are all naturally isomorphic to one another by twisting the line bundles on each component by <math id="S5.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; in
particular, we can identify all of them with a fixed <math id="S5.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
On each <math id="S5.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have a universal line
bundle <math id="S5.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and just as in the first case, we take a very ample
divisor <math id="S5.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 5.2. ‣ 5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> for the <math id="S5.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
twist <math id="S5.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and then construct Grassmannian bundles
<math id="S5.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, this time one for each <math id="S5.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denoting by <math id="S5.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> the product of
all these Grassmannians over <math id="S5.p13.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, we take the closed subscheme inside <math id="S5.p13.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> cut
out by, as in the first case, vanishing on <math id="S5.p13.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and the required
ramification conditions along the <math id="S5.p13.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, we actually write
<math id="S5.p13.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=D^{Y}+D^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=D^{Y}+D^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.p13.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p13.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> are supported on <math id="S5.p13.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p13.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>
respectively, and impose vanishing along <math id="S5.p13.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> only in <math id="S5.p13.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and along
<math id="S5.p13.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> only in <math id="S5.p13.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, we make use of the construction Lemma
<a href="#A1.Thmthm3" title="Lemma A.3. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> to
add the requirement that the <math id="S5.p13.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> each map into <math id="S5.p13.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p13.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p13.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p13.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> under the natural maps, also as in the definition of the
functor. This completes the construction in the second case.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">In the final case, the first step is to work sufficiently locally on <math id="S5.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>
that <math id="S5.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is principal, so that <math id="S5.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{B}(\Delta)\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{B}(\Delta)\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y+Z)\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y+Z)\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and fix a choice of this isomorphism. The rest proceeds very similarly to the second
case: our Picard schemes <math id="S5.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are described identically, but now to
describe isomorphisms between the <math id="S5.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we tensor as necessary by
<math id="S5.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{X}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Replacing the maps between the
<math id="S5.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with the appropriate maps for this case, the rest of the
construction then proceeds identically to the previous case.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Because in each case the construction used only Picard schemes,
Grassmannians, fiber products, and closed subschemes obtained by bounding
the rank of maps between vector bundles, it nearly follows from the standard
representability theorems for these functors that the
<math id="S5.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> scheme we have constructed represents the <math id="S5.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> functor. We do
need to note that in the second and third cases, our conditions for vanishing
along <math id="S5.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> actually imply that all <math id="S5.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> vanish along <math id="S5.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>: in the second
case, this follows simply because <math id="S5.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint from <math id="S5.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; in the third case, we have similarly that <math id="S5.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is disjoint from <math id="S5.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint from <math id="S5.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ 5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, our definition of sub-bundle is
compatible with the usual definition for the Grassmannian functor. We have thus proven representability. It easily follows from the projectivity of Grassmannians and Picard schemes that our <math id="S5.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> scheme is projective over <math id="S5.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Lastly, compatibility with base
change has already been proven in Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 4.8. ‣ 4. The Relative 
        
          
            
            
              
              G
              r
            
            d
          
        
       Functor ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now verify that the moduli scheme we have constructed has the desired
lower bound on its dimension. Our ambient scheme <math id="S5.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a product of
Grassmannians over a Picard scheme, so since <math id="S5.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> was assumed to be regular, we conclude that <math id="S5.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is regular. Hence, in order to bound the codimension
of <math id="S5.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> it suffices to consider the codimensions of
each condition cutting it out. We denote by <math id="S5.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> the rank of <math id="S5.p16.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{P*}\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}^{i}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{P*}\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}^{i}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>; we have <math id="S5.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime\prime}=d+\deg D+1-g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime\prime}=d+\deg D+1-g</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, in the first case, vanishing along <math id="S5.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> imposes <math id="S5.p17.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\operatorname{rk}V)(\operatorname{rk}\pi_{P*}\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}|_{D})=(r+1)(\deg D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>rk</mi><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>rk</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>deg</mi><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\operatorname{rk}V)(\operatorname{rk}\pi_{P*}\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}|_{D})=(r+1)(\deg D)</annotation></semantics></math> conditions. Next, we consider the codimension of the ramification conditions. For this calculation, it suffices to work étale locally, so by Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 5.2. ‣ 5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> we may assume that <math id="S5.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is disjoint from the <math id="S5.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, in which case it follows that ramification conditions imposed
on sub-bundles of <math id="S5.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}(D)</annotation></semantics></math> are equivalent to the desired ramification for
sub-bundles of <math id="S5.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the evaluation maps
<math id="S5.p17.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{P*}\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}(D)\rightarrow\pi_{P*}(\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}(D)|_{jP_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{P*}\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}(D)\rightarrow\pi_{P*}(\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}(D)|_{jP_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math> are
surjective, each condition defines a Schubert cycle. In particular,
by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Thm. 6.3, Cor. 5.12 (b)]</cite> the imposition of ramification at <math id="S5.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> gives an
integral subscheme of codimension <math id="S5.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j}(a^{i}_{j}-j)=\sum_{j}(\alpha^{i}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j}(a^{i}_{j}-j)=\sum_{j}(\alpha^{i}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>
inside <math id="S5.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the total codimension of any component of <math id="S5.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="S5.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
is at most <math id="S5.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+1)(\deg D)+\sum_{j}(\alpha^{i}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+1)(\deg D)+\sum_{j}(\alpha^{i}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">In the second and third cases, the only real difference is that we replace
the Grassmannian with the linked Grassmannian of Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>; it is
easily verified that because the maps on <math id="S5.p18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{P*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{P*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are induced from maps
on the <math id="S5.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, they satisfy the conditions of a linked Grassmannian
(Definition <a href="#A1.Thmthm4" title="Definition A.4. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>). Note that in the case of a reducible fiber,
everything in the kernel of <math id="S5.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> really is in the image of <math id="S5.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and vice versa, because the <math id="S5.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> have all been constructed to be sufficiently ample.
Then it follows from Theorem <a href="#A1.Thmthm15" title="Theorem A.15. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.15</span></a> that every component of the
linked Grassmannian has codimension <math id="S5.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(r+1)(d^{\prime\prime}-r-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(r+1)(d^{\prime\prime}-r-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the rest of the calculation proceeds the same way, with the minor exception
that we have to compute vanishing on <math id="S5.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>
separately and use <math id="S5.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg D^{Y}+\deg D^{Z}=\deg D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg D^{Y}+\deg D^{Z}=\deg D</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we conclude the desired dimension statement, noting that <math id="S5.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is
catenary, and smooth over <math id="S5.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> of relative dimension
<math id="S5.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+1)(d^{\prime\prime}-r-1)+g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+1)(d^{\prime\prime}-r-1)+g</annotation></semantics></math> in the first case and <math id="S5.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d+1)(r+1)(d^{\prime\prime}-r-1)+g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d+1)(r+1)(d^{\prime\prime}-r-1)+g</annotation></semantics></math> in the
second and third cases.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">Lastly, we need to show that the sub-functor of separable limit series is
representable by an open subscheme. Denote by <math id="S5.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathscr{F}}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℱ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathscr{F}}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> the universal sub-bundles of
<math id="S5.p20.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{P*}\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{P*}\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on our <math id="S5.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> scheme. As in the proof of Proposition
<a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 2.4. ‣ 2. Linear Series in Arbitrary Characteristic ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, we can construct a map <math id="S5.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathscr{F}}}^{i}\otimes{\mathscr{O}}_{X\times_{B}G^{r}_{d}}\rightarrow{\mathscr{P}}^{r}(\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℱ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathscr{F}}}^{i}\otimes{\mathscr{O}}_{X\times_{B}G^{r}_{d}}\rightarrow{\mathscr{P}}^{r}(\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{P}}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{P}}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the bundle of
principal parts of order <math id="S5.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>; taking <math id="S5.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+1)</annotation></semantics></math>st exterior powers gives a map
<math id="S5.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\operatorname{univ}}:\det(\tilde{{\mathscr{F}}}^{i})\rightarrow\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}^{\otimes r+1}\otimes(\Omega^{1}_{X/B})^{\otimes\binom{r+1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.111em" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℱ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\operatorname{univ}}:\det(\tilde{{\mathscr{F}}}^{i})\rightarrow\tilde{{\mathscr{L}}}^{\otimes r+1}\otimes(\Omega^{1}_{X/B})^{\otimes\binom{r+1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>; we already noted that in the smooth
case, our separable subscheme is the image under <math id="S5.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> of the complement of the closed subscheme cut out as the kernel of <math id="S5.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\operatorname{univ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\operatorname{univ}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the second and third cases, we restrict to the smooth locus to avoid the problem that <math id="S5.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{1}_{X/B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{1}_{X/B}</annotation></semantics></math> is no longer locally free. It is then easy to check that the same construction applied to <math id="S5.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{F}}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{F}}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{F}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℱ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{F}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> will give the subscheme of linear series which are separable on <math id="S5.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p20.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, and their intersection gives the desired <math id="S5.p20.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> subscheme.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p21.1" class="ltx_p">Our first application is the same regeneration/smoothing theorem due to
Eisenbud-Harris, except that now it <span id="S5.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a priori</span> gives results on
smoothings of
crude limit series as well, and we are also able to include upper bounds of
dimensions of general fibers in certain cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p22.1" class="ltx_p">We have:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the situation of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 5.3. ‣ 5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>,
suppose that <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, that <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is
any open subscheme of our <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> scheme, and that for some point <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in B</annotation></semantics></math>,
the fiber of <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> has the expected dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. Then every
point of the fiber may be smoothed to nearby points. Specifically:</span></p>
<ul id="S5.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The map from </span><math id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to </span><math id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is open at any point in the fiber over
</span><math id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and for any component </span><math id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> whose image contains </span><math id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the generic
fiber of </span><math id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> over </span><math id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has dimension </span><math id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If further </span><math id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is closed in </span><math id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then there is a neighborhood
</span><math id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that the preimage of </span><math id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in </span><math id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is open over </span><math id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and for
each component </span><math id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, every component of
every fiber of </span><math id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> over </span><math id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has dimension precisely </span><math id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I3.ix2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, if <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a curve of compact type (with two components)
over an algebraically closed field, with <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{P}_{1},\dots,\bar{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{P}_{1},\dots,\bar{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
distinct smooth closed points of <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> any collection of
ramification sequences, and <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> any open subset of
<math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X_{0}/k;\{(P_{i},\alpha^{i})\}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X_{0}/k;\{(P_{i},\alpha^{i})\}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> having expected dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, then
there exists a smooth curve <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> over a one-dimensional function field
<math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, specializing to <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, with points <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> specializing to the
<math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{P}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{P}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and such that every point of <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> smooths
to <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>; if further <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{0}=G^{r}_{d}(X_{0}/k;\{(\bar{P}_{i},\alpha^{i})\}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{0}=G^{r}_{d}(X_{0}/k;\{(\bar{P}_{i},\alpha^{i})\}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X_{1}/k^{\prime};\{(P_{i},\alpha^{i})\}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X_{1}/k^{\prime};\{(P_{i},\alpha^{i})\}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> also has dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">For (i), let <math id="S5.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in Z</annotation></semantics></math> be any
closed point in the fiber of <math id="S5.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> the generic point of
<math id="S5.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. Say <math id="S5.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> maps to <math id="S5.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>; then the dimension of the fiber of <math id="S5.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> over
<math id="S5.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> is at most <math id="S5.p23.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, Thm. 13.1.3]</cite>, and at least <math id="S5.p23.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> by Theorem
<a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 5.3. ‣ 5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> after base change to <math id="S5.p23.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>. But because the fiber over <math id="S5.p23.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension <math id="S5.p23.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, and by Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 5.3. ‣ 5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> we have <math id="S5.p23.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim{\mathscr{O}}_{Z,x}\geq\dim{\mathscr{O}}_{B,b}+\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim{\mathscr{O}}_{Z,x}\geq\dim{\mathscr{O}}_{B,b}+\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, the image of <math id="S5.p23.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> cannot have dimension less than <math id="S5.p23.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim{\mathscr{O}}_{B,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim{\mathscr{O}}_{B,b}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S5.p23.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> is the generic point of <math id="S5.p23.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the openness assertion, it suffices to prove that the image of <math id="S5.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>
contains a neighborhood of <math id="S5.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, since if we replace <math id="S5.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> by any neighborhood
of a point of the fiber of <math id="S5.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, the hypotheses of our corollary are
still satisfied. Let <math id="S5.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a point of <math id="S5.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, specializing to <math id="S5.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>; let <math id="S5.p24.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
be the closure of <math id="S5.p24.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p24.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider the base change
<math id="S5.p24.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}\rightarrow B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}\rightarrow B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.p24.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> has codimension <math id="S5.p24.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p24.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, then every
component of <math id="S5.p24.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> would have codimension at most <math id="S5.p24.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p24.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, so if we restrict to a
component <math id="S5.p24.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p24.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> passing through <math id="S5.p24.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S5.p24.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim{\mathscr{O}}_{Z_{1},x}\geq\dim{\mathscr{O}}_{B_{1},b}+\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim{\mathscr{O}}_{Z_{1},x}\geq\dim{\mathscr{O}}_{B_{1},b}+\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, so arguing as before we see that <math id="S5.p24.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> must be in
the image of <math id="S5.p24.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, by constructibility of the image, <math id="S5.p24.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(U)</annotation></semantics></math> must
contain some neighborhood of <math id="S5.p24.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, as desired.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text">For (ii), if <math id="S5.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is closed in <math id="S5.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> we have that it is proper over <math id="S5.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, and
every component <math id="S5.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> either contains <math id="S5.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> in its image, or is supported
on a closed subset of <math id="S5.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> away from <math id="S5.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> maps to <math id="S5.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, we can apply (i)
to conclude that <math id="S5.p25.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> maps surjectively to <math id="S5.p25.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, and by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, Cor.
13.1.5]</cite> the locus on <math id="S5.p25.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> of fibers of <math id="S5.p25.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> having a
component of dimension greater than <math id="S5.p25.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is closed, so taking its
complement and intersecting over the finitely many components of <math id="S5.p25.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> gives
a <math id="S5.p25.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of the desired form. Openness then follows from (i) and the fact that
all fibers over <math id="S5.p25.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> have dimension <math id="S5.p25.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, given an <math id="S5.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as described, we can apply Theorem
<a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 3.4. ‣ 3. Smoothing Families ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> to place <math id="S5.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> into a smoothing family <math id="S5.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/B</annotation></semantics></math> with
generic fiber <math id="S5.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>; the desired assertions then follow immediately from the
main assertions of the corollary.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p27.1" class="ltx_p">The finite case is particularly nice, but we put off any discussion of it
until after we have introduced the language of Eisenbud-Harris limit series
in the next section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p28.1" class="ltx_p">Even without knowing anything about the separable locus being closed, which
in general seems to be a subtle issue, we can still obtain results on
lifting from characteristic <math id="S5.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> to characteristic <math id="S5.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. However, note that the
expected dimension hypothesis in the following corollary is not only key to
the argument, but at least in some cases both non-vacuous and necessary for
the validity of the conclusion. See in particular
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Prop. 5.4, Rem. 8.3]</cite>.
In any case, our machinery now easily yields:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the situation of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 5.3. ‣ 5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, suppose
that <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, that <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is a mixed-characteristic DVR, and that the
special fiber of some <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> open inside <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> has the expected
dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. Then every point
<math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> in the special fiber may be lifted to characteristic
<math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, in the sense that there will be a point <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of the generic fiber
of <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> (and in particular of <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>) specializing to <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, suppose that <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth, proper
curve over a perfect field <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of characteristic <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, with
<math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{P}_{1},\dots,\bar{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{P}_{1},\dots,\bar{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> distinct closed
points of <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> any collection of
ramification sequences, and <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> any open subset of
<math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X_{0}/k;\{(\bar{P}_{i},\alpha^{i})\}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X_{0}/k;\{(\bar{P}_{i},\alpha^{i})\}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> having expected dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>;
then there exists a smooth curve <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> over the fraction field <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of the Witt
vectors of <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, specializing to <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, with points <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> specializing to the
<math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{P}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{P}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and such that every point of <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> may be lifted to a point of
<math id="S5.Thmthm5.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X_{1}/K;\{(P_{i},\alpha^{i})\}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X_{1}/K;\{(P_{i},\alpha^{i})\}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first assertion follows immediately from the openness proven in
Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 5.4. ‣ 5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the second assertion, let <math id="S5.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be the Witt vectors of <math id="S5.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>; then by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, 11, Thm 1.1]</cite> we can find an <math id="S5.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}A</annotation></semantics></math> whose special
fiber is <math id="S5.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and since <math id="S5.p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is complete and the <math id="S5.p30.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{P}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{P}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth
points we can lift them to sections <math id="S5.p30.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p30.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying the first
assertion then gives the desired result.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 5.6</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This last corollary, dealing only with smooth curves, has nothing to do with limit linear series, and only uses the elementary lower bound on the dimension of a standard <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> space with imposed ramification.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is all well and good, but our description of the limit series
associated to a reducible curve is rather cumbersome, so we now establish
the relationship to Eisenbud and Harris’ limit series in this situation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_sit">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Situation 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/B</annotation></semantics></math> is a smoothing family with <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
reducible; specifically, falling into case (2) of Situation <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="Situation 4.4. ‣ 4. The Relative 
        
          
            
            
              
              G
              r
            
            d
          
        
       Functor ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first step is to consider the “forgetful” map from our
<math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math> functor into the product of <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}(Y/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}(Y/B)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}(Z/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{r}_{d}(Z/B)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We have:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In Situation <a href="#S6.Thmthm1" title="Situation 6.1. ‣ 6. Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>, given any <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued point <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{({\mathscr{L}}^{i},V_{i})\}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{({\mathscr{L}}^{i},V_{i})\}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math>, the pair <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(({\mathscr{L}}^{0}|_{Y_{T}},V_{0}|_{Y_{T}}),({\mathscr{L}}^{d}|_{Z_{T}},V_{d}|_{Z_{T}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(({\mathscr{L}}^{0}|_{Y_{T}},V_{0}|_{Y_{T}}),({\mathscr{L}}^{d}|_{Z_{T}},V_{d}|_{Z_{T}}))</annotation></semantics></math> gives a <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued point of
<math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(Y/B)\times G^{r}_{d}(Z/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(Y/B)\times G^{r}_{d}(Z/B)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular,
this defines a morphism
<math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FR:G^{r}_{d}(X/B)\rightarrow G^{r}_{d}(Y/B)\times_{B}G^{r}_{d}(Z/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FR:G^{r}_{d}(X/B)\rightarrow G^{r}_{d}(Y/B)\times_{B}G^{r}_{d}(Z/B)</annotation></semantics></math>. A limit series
in <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math> is separable if and only if its image under <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FR" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FR</annotation></semantics></math> is
separable in both <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(Y/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(Y/B)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(Z/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(Z/B)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">It is clearly enough to show that given <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T/B</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{({\mathscr{L}}^{i},V_{i})\}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{({\mathscr{L}}^{i},V_{i})\}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued point of <math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}|_{Y_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}|_{Y_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sub-bundle of <math id="S6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{0}|_{Y_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{0}|_{Y_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> and correspondingly for
<math id="S6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. This follows immediately from our definition of sub-bundle, since if a section of <math id="S6.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0S}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes on <math id="S6.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S6.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S6.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, it defines a section of the negative line bundle <math id="S6.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{0}|_{Z}(-\Delta^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{0}|_{Z}(-\Delta^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and hence vanishes on <math id="S6.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> as well, and similarly for <math id="S6.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{dS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{dS}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> switched.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The statement on separability is immediate from the definition of
separability of a limit series on a reducible curve.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_notn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Notation 6.3</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the same situation as the previous lemma, we denote the image under <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FR" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FR</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{({\mathscr{L}}^{i},V_{i})\}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{({\mathscr{L}}^{i},V_{i})\}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(({\mathscr{L}}^{Y},V^{Y}),({\mathscr{L}}^{Z},V^{Z}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(({\mathscr{L}}^{Y},V^{Y}),({\mathscr{L}}^{Z},V^{Z}))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further, we denote by
<math id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> the image of <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}({\mathscr{L}}^{i}|_{Y})\cong\pi_{*}({\mathscr{L}}^{Y}(-i\Delta^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}({\mathscr{L}}^{i}|_{Y})\cong\pi_{*}({\mathscr{L}}^{Y}(-i\Delta^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly for <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.4</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the same situation as the previous lemma,
we have the following additional observations (and consequent notation):</span></p>
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> injects naturally into </span><math id="S6.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and similarly for
</span><math id="S6.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> will be contained in </span><math id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}\subset V^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}\subset V^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
where </span><math id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\beta^{Y}_{i}:V^{Y}\rightarrow\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}|_{i\Delta^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{Y}_{i}:V^{Y}\rightarrow\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}|_{i\Delta^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the natural
</span><math id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">th order evaluation map at </span><math id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> will
similarly be contained in </span><math id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}\subset V^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}\subset V^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.ix2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The induced map </span><math id="S6.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}\rightarrow V^{Y}\oplus V^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}\rightarrow V^{Y}\oplus V^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in fact
exhibits </span><math id="S6.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as a sub-bundle (in the usual sense) of </span><math id="S6.I1.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}\oplus V^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}\oplus V^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.ix3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assertions (i) and (ii) are clear. For (iii), it suffices to show that
<math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}\rightarrow V^{Y}\oplus V^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}\rightarrow V^{Y}\oplus V^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective after any base change <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\rightarrow T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\rightarrow T</annotation></semantics></math>, and this is easily verified from the definitions.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.5</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In case (2) of Situation <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="Situation 4.4. ‣ 4. The Relative 
        
          
            
            
              
              G
              r
            
            d
          
        
       Functor ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, we define an
<span id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Eisenbud-Harris (crude) limit series</span> on <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to be a
pair <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(({\mathscr{L}}^{Y},V^{Y}),({\mathscr{L}}^{Z},V^{Z}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(({\mathscr{L}}^{Y},V^{Y}),({\mathscr{L}}^{Z},V^{Z}))</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(Y)\times_{B}G^{r}_{d}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(Y)\times_{B}G^{r}_{d}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying
<math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{i}(\Delta^{\prime})+a^{Z}_{r-i}(\Delta^{\prime})\geq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{i}(\Delta^{\prime})+a^{Z}_{r-i}(\Delta^{\prime})\geq d</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> (see below). The
closed subscheme
of <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(Y)\times_{B}G^{r}_{d}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(Y)\times_{B}G^{r}_{d}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by these ramifications conditions
will be denoted
<math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math>. We also define <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)\subset G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)\subset G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math> to be the
open subscheme of limit series which are separable on each component, and
<math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ref</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> to be the open subscheme of <span id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">refined</span>
Eisenbud-Harris
limit series satisfying <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{i}(\Delta^{\prime})+a^{Z}_{r-i}(\Delta^{\prime})=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{i}(\Delta^{\prime})+a^{Z}_{r-i}(\Delta^{\prime})=d</annotation></semantics></math> for all
<math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, or more
precisely, the complement of the closed subscheme satisfying
<math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{i}(\Delta^{\prime})+a^{Z}_{r-i}(\Delta^{\prime})&gt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{i}(\Delta^{\prime})+a^{Z}_{r-i}(\Delta^{\prime})&gt;d</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We remark that these ramification conditions do in fact
give a canonical closed subscheme structure:
for each sequence of <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math> non-decreasing integers <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq a_{i}\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq a_{i}\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>, we
get a closed subscheme defined by the conditions
<math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{i}(\Delta^{\prime})\geq a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{i}(\Delta^{\prime})\geq a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Z}_{r-i}(\Delta^{\prime})\geq d-a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Z}_{r-i}(\Delta^{\prime})\geq d-a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>; there
are only finitely many such sequences, so the union of the closed subschemes
obtained over each of them is again a closed subscheme. However, this
definition gives us trouble when we attempt to show that our
<math id="S6.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math> maps into <math id="S6.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math>, as it is difficult to describe
the <math id="S6.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued points of a union of schemes in terms of the <math id="S6.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued
points of the individual schemes. As a result, we settle for the following slightly weaker statement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.6</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have the following facts about the image of
<math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FR:G^{r}_{d}(X/B)\rightarrow G^{r}_{d}(Y/B)\times_{B}G^{r}_{d}(Z/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FR:G^{r}_{d}(X/B)\rightarrow G^{r}_{d}(Y/B)\times_{B}G^{r}_{d}(Z/B)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ul id="S6.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FR" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FR</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I2.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has set-theoretic image precisely </span><math id="S6.I2.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I2.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I2.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Scheme-theoretically, </span><math id="S6.I2.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I2.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> maps into the closed subscheme
satisfying for all </span><math id="S6.I2.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S6.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{j}(\Delta^{\prime})+a^{Z}_{r-j}(\Delta^{\prime})\geq d-1;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{j}(\Delta^{\prime})+a^{Z}_{r-j}(\Delta^{\prime})\geq d-1;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I2.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The open subscheme of </span><math id="S6.I2.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I2.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> mapping set-theoretically into
</span><math id="S6.I2.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ref</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I2.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> actually maps scheme-theoretically into
</span><math id="S6.I2.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X)\subset G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ref</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X)\subset G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I2.ix3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">In general, for a <math id="S6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued pair <math id="S6.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(({\mathscr{L}}^{Y},V^{Y}),({\mathscr{L}}^{Z},V^{Z}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(({\mathscr{L}}^{Y},V^{Y}),({\mathscr{L}}^{Z},V^{Z}))</annotation></semantics></math>,
define <math id="S6.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the largest
integer <math id="S6.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Y}_{i}\leq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Y}_{i}\leq j</annotation></semantics></math> everywhere on <math id="S6.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly
for <math id="S6.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. The set-theoretic statement may be checked point by point, and is
equivalent to saying that when <math id="S6.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\operatorname{Spec}k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\operatorname{Spec}k</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S6.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S6.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(({\mathscr{L}}^{Y},V^{Y}),({\mathscr{L}}^{Z},V^{Z}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(({\mathscr{L}}^{Y},V^{Y}),({\mathscr{L}}^{Z},V^{Z}))</annotation></semantics></math> is
in the image of <math id="S6.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FR" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FR</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S6.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}^{Y}+a_{r-j}^{Z}\geq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}^{Y}+a_{r-j}^{Z}\geq d</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
For (ii), it is enough to check that for arbitrary local
<math id="S6.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}^{Y}+a_{r-j}^{Z}\geq d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}^{Y}+a_{r-j}^{Z}\geq d-1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, and for (iii), we want to show in this case that if the
point obtained by restriction to the closed point of <math id="S6.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies
<math id="S6.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}^{Y}+a_{r-j}^{Z}=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}^{Y}+a_{r-j}^{Z}=d</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, then the entire <math id="S6.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued point does.
In all cases, we make use of the fact from Lemma
<a href="#S6.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 6.4. ‣ 6. Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a> that <math id="S6.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> may be considered as lying inside
<math id="S6.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conceptually,
the basic idea is
that for <math id="S6.p8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to maintain rank <math id="S6.p8.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math> at each <math id="S6.p8.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, the ranks of <math id="S6.p8.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p8.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}</annotation></semantics></math> must add up to at least <math id="S6.p8.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and
looking at <math id="S6.p8.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=a^{Y}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=a^{Y}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for different <math id="S6.p8.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> should yield the desired
inequalities. As we will see, this works over a field, but is not quite so
nice for a more general <math id="S6.p8.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, for the set-theoretic statement (i), suppose we have a
<math id="S6.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-valued point <math id="S6.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{({\mathscr{L}}^{i},V_{i})\}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{({\mathscr{L}}^{i},V_{i})\}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math>; we
first show that it maps into <math id="S6.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S6.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is glued
from subspaces of <math id="S6.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}</annotation></semantics></math>
and has dimension <math id="S6.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math>, we conclude that <math id="S6.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}+\dim\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}\geq r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}+\dim\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}\geq r+1</annotation></semantics></math>,
so <math id="S6.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Y}_{i}+\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Z}_{d-i}\leq r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Y}_{i}+\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Z}_{d-i}\leq r+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and
it follows that <math id="S6.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}^{Y}+a_{r+1-j}^{Z}\geq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}^{Y}+a_{r+1-j}^{Z}\geq d</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, for a given <math id="S6.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, set <math id="S6.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=a_{j}^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=a_{j}^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>; we
know that <math id="S6.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Y}_{i+1}&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Y}_{i+1}&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, so one of the sections in <math id="S6.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is
non-vanishing at <math id="S6.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> when considered as a section of <math id="S6.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{i}(-i\Delta^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{i}(-i\Delta^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>,
and to use it in <math id="S6.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, it must be glued to a section of <math id="S6.p9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
similarly non-vanishing at <math id="S6.p9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S6.p9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim V_{i}&lt;\dim\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}+\dim\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim V_{i}&lt;\dim\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}+\dim\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}</annotation></semantics></math>, so our earlier argument
gives <math id="S6.p9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Y}_{i}+\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Z}_{d-i}&lt;r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Y}_{i}+\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Z}_{d-i}&lt;r+1</annotation></semantics></math>, hence
<math id="S6.p9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}^{Y}+a_{r-j}^{Z}\geq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}^{Y}+a_{r-j}^{Z}\geq d</annotation></semantics></math>. For later use, note that when
<math id="S6.p9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{j}+a^{Z}_{r-j}=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{j}+a^{Z}_{r-j}=d</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="S6.p9.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, this argument shows that we have <math id="S6.p9.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}=V^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}=V^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.p9.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}=V^{Z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}=V^{Z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p9.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and in particular when <math id="S6.p9.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=a^{Y}_{j}=d-a^{Z}_{r-j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=a^{Y}_{j}=d-a^{Z}_{r-j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get <math id="S6.p9.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim V^{Y}_{i}+\dim V^{Z}_{i}=r+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim V^{Y}_{i}+\dim V^{Z}_{i}=r+2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, given a <math id="S6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(({\mathscr{L}}^{Y},V^{Y}),({\mathscr{L}}^{Z},V^{Z}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(({\mathscr{L}}^{Y},V^{Y}),({\mathscr{L}}^{Z},V^{Z}))</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the Eisenbud-Harris inequalities, we
construct the <math id="S6.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by
gluing <math id="S6.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}(-i\Delta^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}(-i\Delta^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{Z}((i-d)\Delta^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{Z}((i-d)\Delta^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, and set <math id="S6.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}=V^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}=V^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S6.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}=V^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}=V^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that sections in <math id="S6.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> which vanish at <math id="S6.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are
extended by <math id="S6.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S6.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. If there is a non-vanishing section, then
<math id="S6.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S6.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Z}_{r}\geq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Z}_{r}\geq d</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is necessarily
<math id="S6.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{Z_{T}}(d\Delta^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{Z_{T}}(d\Delta^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S6.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{i}|_{Z}\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{Z_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{i}|_{Z}\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{Z_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we can (uniquely)
extend sections not vanishing at <math id="S6.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, also. We also observe that this
implies that we have <math id="S6.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> mapping into <math id="S6.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> under iterations of <math id="S6.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. By
symmetry, we can make the same arguments for <math id="S6.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> to get our <math id="S6.p10.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now we inductively construct each <math id="S6.p10.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.p10.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\dots,d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\dots,d-1</annotation></semantics></math> in
terms of <math id="S6.p10.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p10.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our induction hypothesis will be that <math id="S6.p10.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is linked to the previous <math id="S6.p10.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p10.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> under iterates of <math id="S6.p10.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p10.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and furthermore that each <math id="S6.p10.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> has a basis of sections each
of which is either non-vanishing at <math id="S6.p10.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, or vanishes uniformly on
either <math id="S6.p10.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p10.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, with at most one basis element in the first category. We denote the number of each of
these by <math id="S6.p10.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p10.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p10.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, where we have <math id="S6.p10.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
always <math id="S6.p10.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p10.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p10.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}^{1}+r_{j}^{2}+r_{j}^{3}=r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}^{1}+r_{j}^{2}+r_{j}^{3}=r+1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p10.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, we also impose in our induction hypothesis that <math id="S6.p10.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is always the maximal
possible value, which is <math id="S6.p10.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\ker\beta^{Y}_{j+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\ker\beta^{Y}_{j+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that since this is
non-decreasing, if we construct a <math id="S6.p10.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p10.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}^{3}=r_{i-1}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}^{3}=r_{i-1}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, maximality
is automatically satisfied.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, for general <math id="S6.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, suppose we have constructed the <math id="S6.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> up to <math id="S6.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfying our induction hypothesis. To construct <math id="S6.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the basis elements
vanishing on <math id="S6.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> must contain <math id="S6.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is an
<math id="S6.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r_{i-1}^{1}+r_{i-1}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r_{i-1}^{1}+r_{i-1}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space, and of course they must map into
<math id="S6.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S6.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math> maps into <math id="S6.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can choose
<math id="S6.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i-1}^{1}+r_{i-1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i-1}^{1}+r_{i-1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such sections, by taking any basis of <math id="S6.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, the basis elements vanishing on <math id="S6.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> must be contained in
<math id="S6.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i-1}^{-1}(V_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i-1}^{-1}(V_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, and we choose them to be a basis of the
subspace of <math id="S6.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i-1}^{-1}(V_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i-1}^{-1}(V_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishing on <math id="S6.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is at most an <math id="S6.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i-1}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i-1}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space, with equality if all of
the <math id="S6.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i-1}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i-1}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> basis elements of <math id="S6.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> vanish to order greater than one
at <math id="S6.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. If there was a section vanishing
to order exactly one at <math id="S6.p11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i-1}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i-1}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> will instead be
<math id="S6.p11.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r_{i-1}^{3}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r_{i-1}^{3}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional. Now, by our induction hypothesis, the
iterated image of <math id="S6.p11.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> under the <math id="S6.p11.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in <math id="S6.p11.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
necessarily in the span of the basis elements vanishing on <math id="S6.p11.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover,
since <math id="S6.p11.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>, this image lies in the subspace of <math id="S6.p11.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishing
to order at least <math id="S6.p11.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S6.p11.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, so it
is automatically contained in the span of the basis elements we have
chosen for <math id="S6.p11.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> which vanish on <math id="S6.p11.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, if we had <math id="S6.p11.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i-1}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i-1}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> such
basis elements, we are done. If not, we had a section of <math id="S6.p11.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishing
on <math id="S6.p11.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> and vanishing to first order at <math id="S6.p11.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p11.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, so it follows that
<math id="S6.p11.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}=\dim\ker\beta^{Y}_{i+1}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}=\dim\ker\beta^{Y}_{i+1}+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and therefore that
<math id="S6.p11.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{r+1-r^{3}_{i-1}}=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{r+1-r^{3}_{i-1}}=i</annotation></semantics></math>; in particular, the required maximality of <math id="S6.p11.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{3}_{i}=r^{3}_{i-1}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{3}_{i}=r^{3}_{i-1}-1</annotation></semantics></math>
is satisfied. It also follows that <math id="S6.p11.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Z}_{r^{3}_{i-1}-1}\geq d-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Z}_{r^{3}_{i-1}-1}\geq d-i</annotation></semantics></math>; if it is
equal, we can find a section of <math id="S6.p11.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishing to order precisely <math id="S6.p11.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-i</annotation></semantics></math> at
<math id="S6.p11.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we could glue to our final section of <math id="S6.p11.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i-1}^{-1}(V_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i-1}^{-1}(V_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain our <math id="S6.p11.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+1)</annotation></semantics></math>st generator for <math id="S6.p11.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which will be
non-vanishing at <math id="S6.p11.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise, we have <math id="S6.p11.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Z}_{r^{3}_{i-1}-1}&gt;d-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Z}_{r^{3}_{i-1}-1}&gt;d-i</annotation></semantics></math>,
so following through the definitions, <math id="S6.p11.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i+1}\geq r+2-r^{3}_{i-1}=1+r^{1}_{i-1}+r^{2}_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i+1}\geq r+2-r^{3}_{i-1}=1+r^{1}_{i-1}+r^{2}_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we can choose an
<math id="S6.p11.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r^{1}_{i-1}+r^{2}_{i-1}+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r^{1}_{i-1}+r^{2}_{i-1}+1)</annotation></semantics></math>st generator vanishing on <math id="S6.p11.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> to be our <math id="S6.p11.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+1)</annotation></semantics></math>st
generator for <math id="S6.p11.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. This completes the proof of the set-theoretic
surjectivity of <math id="S6.p11.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FR" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FR</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S6.p11.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the scheme-theoretic statements, let <math id="S6.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\operatorname{Spec}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\operatorname{Spec}A</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is any local
ring with maximal ideal <math id="S6.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 6.4. ‣ 6. Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>, for each
<math id="S6.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> we have
<math id="S6.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow V_{i}\rightarrow V^{Y}\oplus V^{Z}\rightarrow Q\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow V_{i}\rightarrow V^{Y}\oplus V^{Z}\rightarrow Q\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>
for some free <math id="S6.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Working modulo <math id="S6.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> and
then using Nakayama’s lemma, we find that for some <math id="S6.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> depending on <math id="S6.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we
can construct
sub-bundles of <math id="S6.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> whose images in <math id="S6.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are sub-bundles of
<math id="S6.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S6.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1-j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1-j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. These are contained in <math id="S6.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
we conclude that <math id="S6.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{j}+a^{Z}_{r+1-j}\geq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{j}+a^{Z}_{r+1-j}\geq d</annotation></semantics></math>. As in the fields case, if
we set <math id="S6.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=a^{Y}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=a^{Y}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, then by hypothesis <math id="S6.p12.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Y}_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Y}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is not
less than or equal to <math id="S6.p12.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> on all of <math id="S6.p12.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, so our constructed sub-bundle of
<math id="S6.p12.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.p12.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1</annotation></semantics></math> could have rank at most <math id="S6.p12.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-j</annotation></semantics></math>, and the sub-bundle of <math id="S6.p12.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>
would have
to have rank at least <math id="S6.p12.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j+1</annotation></semantics></math>, giving <math id="S6.p12.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Z}_{d-i-1}\leq r-j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}\beta^{Z}_{d-i-1}\leq r-j</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p12.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>,
and yielding the inequality <math id="S6.p12.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{j}+a^{Z}_{r-j}\geq d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{j}+a^{Z}_{r-j}\geq d-1</annotation></semantics></math> of statement (ii).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, for statement (iii), we need only combine this argument with our
earlier observation that at the closed point, where by hypothesis we had
<math id="S6.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{j}+a^{Z}_{r-j}=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{j}+a^{Z}_{r-j}=d</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, when <math id="S6.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=a^{Y}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=a^{Y}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> we necessarily have
<math id="S6.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim V^{Y}_{i}+\dim V^{Z}_{i}=r+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim V^{Y}_{i}+\dim V^{Z}_{i}=r+2</annotation></semantics></math>; thus we can in fact choose a basis of
<math id="S6.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (still modulo <math id="S6.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>) which has rank <math id="S6.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1-j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1-j</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and rank <math id="S6.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j+1</annotation></semantics></math>
(rather than <math id="S6.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>) in <math id="S6.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we then get the desired inequality
<math id="S6.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{j}+a^{Z}_{r+1-j}\geq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{j}+a^{Z}_{r+1-j}\geq d</annotation></semantics></math> for the entire <math id="S6.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued point, as desired.
Note that the subscheme of <math id="S6.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> in question is open simply because
<math id="S6.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is known to map set-theoretically into <math id="S6.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S6.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ref</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is open inside <math id="S6.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p14.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that for a crude Eisenbud-Harris limit series, there may be many
ways of filling in the intermediate <math id="S6.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S6.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the fiber
of <math id="S6.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FR" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FR</annotation></semantics></math> may be positive-dimensional. However, the situation is easier to
get a handle on for the open subset of
refined series which Eisenbud and Harris actually used in their construction.
Indeed, we show that the space of refined limit series is actually isomorphic
to an open subscheme of our <math id="S6.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> scheme.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.7</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}}^{Y},V^{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}}^{Y},V^{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}}^{Z},V^{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}}^{Z},V^{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> form a <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued point of
<math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ref</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have that
<math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}}^{Y},V^{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}}^{Y},V^{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathscr{L}}^{Z},V^{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathscr{L}}^{Z},V^{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> are the image of a unique <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued point
under <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FR" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FR</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">It clearly suffices to handle the case that <math id="S6.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is connected,
so we make this hypothesis. In this case, we see that we get unique
vanishing sequences at <math id="S6.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, in the sense that some sequence is satisfied everywhere on <math id="S6.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, with no stronger ramification index satisfied anywhere on <math id="S6.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, the subscheme of <math id="S6.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S6.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{i}(\Delta^{\prime})+a^{Z}_{r-i}(\Delta^{\prime})&gt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{i}(\Delta^{\prime})+a^{Z}_{r-i}(\Delta^{\prime})&gt;d</annotation></semantics></math> is empty by hypothesis, and because ramification conditions are closed, the ramification sequences obtained at any point of <math id="S6.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> persist in an open and closed neighborhood.
Now, if <math id="S6.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the evaluation map <math id="S6.p15.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V^{Y}\rightarrow\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}|_{i\Delta^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}\rightarrow\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}|_{i\Delta^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and similarly for <math id="S6.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{Z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{Z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
this immediately implies that each <math id="S6.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{Z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{Z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
has rank determined exactly by the vanishing sequences, in the strong sense
that for some <math id="S6.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, the closed subscheme where the rank is less than or equal
to <math id="S6.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is all of <math id="S6.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, but the closed subscheme where the rank is strictly
less than <math id="S6.p15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is empty. It follows (see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Prop. 20.8]</cite>) that the images of the <math id="S6.p15.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p15.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{Z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{Z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are locally free, with
locally free quotients. If we denote by <math id="S6.p15.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{j}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{j}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> the vanishing sequence
at <math id="S6.p15.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.p15.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, we also note that <math id="S6.p15.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> will have
rank <math id="S6.p15.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1-j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1-j</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S6.p15.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j-1}&lt;i\leq a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j-1}&lt;i\leq a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and following through the
definitions we see that <math id="S6.p15.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}</annotation></semantics></math>
will have rank <math id="S6.p15.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S6.p15.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j-1}\leq i&lt;a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j-1}\leq i&lt;a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we find that
<math id="S6.p15.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}=\ker\beta^{Y}_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}=\ker\beta^{Y}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
<math id="S6.p15.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}=\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}=\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S6.p15.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}+\operatorname{rk}\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i+1}=r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}+\operatorname{rk}\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i+1}=r+1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p15.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">The main idea is to construct the <math id="S6.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as the
subspace of <math id="S6.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}\oplus\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}\oplus\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}</annotation></semantics></math> which agree on the
two maps given by evaluation at <math id="S6.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. This would then
be unique by Lemmas <a href="#S6.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 6.4. ‣ 6. Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ 5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>,
so we need only show existence. We work locally on the base, so
that <math id="S6.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}(-i\Delta^{\prime})|_{\Delta^{\prime}}\cong{\mathscr{L}}^{Z}(-i^{\prime}\Delta^{\prime})|_{\Delta^{\prime}}\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{\Delta^{\prime}}\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}(-i\Delta^{\prime})|_{\Delta^{\prime}}\cong{\mathscr{L}}^{Z}(-i^{\prime}\Delta^{\prime})|_{\Delta^{\prime}}\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{\Delta^{\prime}}\cong{\mathscr{O}}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,i^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,i^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
fix a
choice of these isomorphisms. As prescribed for gluing together line bundles
defined on components, we define <math id="S6.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by the short exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow{\mathscr{L}}^{i}\rightarrow{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}(-i\Delta^{\prime})\oplus{\mathscr{L}}^{Z}((i-d)\Delta^{\prime})\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{\Delta^{\prime}}\rightarrow 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow{\mathscr{L}}^{i}\rightarrow{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}(-i\Delta^{\prime})\oplus{\mathscr{L}}^{Z}((i-d)\Delta^{\prime})\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{\Delta^{\prime}}\rightarrow 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p16.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p16.2.1" class="ltx_text">and pushforward gives us</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0\rightarrow\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}\rightarrow\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}(-i\Delta^{\prime})\oplus\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Z}((i-d)\Delta^{\prime})\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{T}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}\rightarrow\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}(-i\Delta^{\prime})\oplus\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Z}((i-d)\Delta^{\prime})\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p16.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p16.3.1" class="ltx_text">We then define <math id="S6.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the kernel of the induced map, so that:</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow V_{i}\rightarrow\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}\oplus\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{T}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow V_{i}\rightarrow\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}\oplus\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have to show that <math id="S6.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sub-bundle of <math id="S6.p17.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of the correct
rank. We first observe that the image <math id="S6.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{Y}_{i+1}(\ker\beta^{Y}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{Y}_{i+1}(\ker\beta^{Y}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> has
locally free quotient in <math id="S6.p17.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}|_{(i+1)\Delta^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}|_{(i+1)\Delta^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly for
<math id="S6.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>: this image is inside <math id="S6.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}\beta^{Y}_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}\beta^{Y}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> by definition, and the
quotient is easily seen to be isomorphic to <math id="S6.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}\beta^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}\beta^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, via
the map <math id="S6.p17.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}|_{(i+1)\Delta^{\prime}}\rightarrow\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}|_{i\Delta^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}|_{(i+1)\Delta^{\prime}}\rightarrow\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}|_{i\Delta^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S6.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{Y}_{i+1}(\ker\beta^{Y}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{Y}_{i+1}(\ker\beta^{Y}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is a sub-bundle of a sub-bundle, and
must itself be a sub-bundle of <math id="S6.p17.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}|_{(i+1)\Delta^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}|_{(i+1)\Delta^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, we can
factor <math id="S6.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{Y}_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{Y}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> restricted to <math id="S6.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}\rightarrow\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}(-i\Delta^{\prime})|_{\Delta^{\prime}}\hookrightarrow\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}|_{(i+1)\Delta^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}\rightarrow\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}(-i\Delta^{\prime})|_{\Delta^{\prime}}\hookrightarrow\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}|_{(i+1)\Delta^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p17.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p17.2.1" class="ltx_text">and we just showed that the cokernel of the composition is locally free;
since <math id="S6.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}(-i\Delta^{\prime})|_{\Delta^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}(-i\Delta^{\prime})|_{\Delta^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle,
this means the first map must be either zero or surjective, with
surjectivity precisely when <math id="S6.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}=\operatorname{rk}\ker\beta^{Y}_{i+1}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}=\operatorname{rk}\ker\beta^{Y}_{i+1}+1</annotation></semantics></math>,
and
the ranks equal otherwise. We obtain the corresponding result for <math id="S6.p17.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and
immediately conclude that <math id="S6.p17.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sub-bundle of <math id="S6.p17.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}\oplus\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}\oplus\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}</annotation></semantics></math>, with equality if and only if both <math id="S6.p17.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}=\ker\beta^{Y}_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Y}_{i}=\ker\beta^{Y}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p17.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}=\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i}=\ker\beta^{Z}_{d-i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
corank one otherwise. Thus, our hypotheses imply that <math id="S6.p17.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has rank <math id="S6.p17.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
The last observation is that <math id="S6.p17.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> being a sub-bundle of <math id="S6.p17.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}\oplus V^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}\oplus V^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>
implies
that it is a sub-bundle (in our generalized sense) of <math id="S6.p17.m24" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, but this
follows easily from the fact that <math id="S6.p17.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p17.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> are sub-bundles of <math id="S6.p17.m27" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p17.m28" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T*}{\mathscr{L}}^{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p18.1" class="ltx_p">We immediately conclude:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.8</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The map
<math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FR:G^{r}_{d}(X/B)\rightarrow G^{r}_{d}(Y/B)\times G^{r}_{d}(Z/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FR:G^{r}_{d}(X/B)\rightarrow G^{r}_{d}(Y/B)\times G^{r}_{d}(Z/B)</annotation></semantics></math>
induces an isomorphism from an open subscheme
<math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ref</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math>, and on the corresponding
separable subschemes of these.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p19.1" class="ltx_p">We may therefore think of the scheme of refined Eisenbud-Harris limit series
as forming an open subscheme of our <math id="S6.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> scheme itself:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.9</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say that a point of <math id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">refined</span> limit
series if it maps under <math id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FR" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FR</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ref</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we denote the
open subscheme of refined limit series by <math id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d}\subset G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ref</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d}\subset G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p20.1" class="ltx_p">We also have the following trivial observation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.10</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 6.2. ‣ 6. Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>, Propositions <a href="#S6.Thmthm6" title="Proposition 6.6. ‣ 6. Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a> and <a href="#S6.Thmthm7" title="Proposition 6.7. ‣ 6. Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.7</span></a>, and Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmthm8" title="Corollary 6.8. ‣ 6. Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.8</span></a> all hold when ramification conditions are imposed.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Indeed, we specified ramification solely
on <math id="S6.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> depending on whether the relevant section was on <math id="S6.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S6.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, so the ramification conditions are visibly compatible with <math id="S6.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FR" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FR</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p22.1" class="ltx_p">Since in practice it is less cumbersome to work with Eisenbud-Harris
series on a given reducible curve, we state our main corollary for the
finite case of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 5.3. ‣ 5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> in a situation where one can (nearly)
restrict attention entirely to the Eisenbud-Harris series. We now drop the
hypothesis that we are in case (2), and for notational convenience define:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.11</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In case (1), i.e., with <math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/B</annotation></semantics></math> smooth, we simply define
<math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math> to be equal to
<math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math> and similarly for <math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ref</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.12</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the situation of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 5.3. ‣ 5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>,
suppose that <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\operatorname{Spec}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\operatorname{Spec}A</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> a
DVR having algebraically closed residue field, and <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and denote by
<math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> the special and generic fibers of <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/B</annotation></semantics></math>. We omit the
ramification conditions from our notation for the sake of brevity. Then
consider the following conditions:</span></p>
<ul id="S6.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(I)</span> 
<div id="S6.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X_{0})\subset G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ref</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X_{0})\subset G^{r,\operatorname{ref}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X_{0})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(II)</span> 
<div id="S6.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I3.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X_{0})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> consists of </span><math id="S6.I3.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> reduced points for some
</span><math id="S6.I3.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I3.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(III)</span> 
<div id="S6.I3.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any DVR </span><math id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and any </span><math id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-valued point of </span><math id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
such that the induced map </span><math id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}A^{\prime}\rightarrow\operatorname{Spec}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}A^{\prime}\rightarrow\operatorname{Spec}A</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is flat and the
generic point of </span><math id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> maps into </span><math id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}(X)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then the closed
point of </span><math id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> maps into </span><math id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}(X)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.ix3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as well.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If (I) and (II) hold, we have that the <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}(X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}(X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> geometrically
contains at least <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> points; if further (III) holds, then
<math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is finite étale over <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, and the
geometric generic fiber also consists of exactly <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> reduced points.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, we have by virtue of (I) and Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmthm8" title="Corollary 6.8. ‣ 6. Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.8</span></a>
that <math id="S6.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X_{0})\cong G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}(X_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X_{0})\cong G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}(X_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. Setting <math id="S6.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>sep</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=G^{r,\operatorname{sep}}_{d}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math>, if we choose any
point <math id="S6.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in the special fiber,
applying Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 5.4. ‣ 5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> we find
that any component <math id="S6.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> passing through <math id="S6.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> maps dominantly to <math id="S6.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S6.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional generic fiber. To count the number of
points, we can take <math id="S6.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> to be reduced, in which case it is
flat over <math id="S6.p23.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, and we obtain the first assertion.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">In the case that (III) holds, we claim that <math id="S6.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is in fact
proper over <math id="S6.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. We begin by noting that the generic fiber must be
<math id="S6.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional: by the preceding arguments, this will follow if we show
that every component <math id="S6.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> meets the special fiber of <math id="S6.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, and this
follows from the properness of <math id="S6.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math> and condition (III).
Now, it suffices to show that <math id="S6.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is closed in <math id="S6.p24.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, so
choose <math id="S6.p24.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in U</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p24.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}\in G^{r}_{d}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}\in G^{r}_{d}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> distinct points with <math id="S6.p24.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> specializing to
<math id="S6.p24.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Prop. 7.1.9]</cite> we can find a DVR <math id="S6.p24.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and a map <math id="S6.p24.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}A^{\prime}\rightarrow G^{r}_{d}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}A^{\prime}\rightarrow G^{r}_{d}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> with the generic point mapping to <math id="S6.p24.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and the
special point mapping to <math id="S6.p24.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The image of <math id="S6.p24.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be contained
in either the special or generic fiber by <math id="S6.p24.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensionality. Therefore, it
gives a flat map <math id="S6.p24.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}A^{\prime}\rightarrow\operatorname{Spec}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}A^{\prime}\rightarrow\operatorname{Spec}A</annotation></semantics></math>, and
hypothesis (III) implies <math id="S6.p24.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}\in U</annotation></semantics></math> as well, yielding properness of <math id="S6.p24.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given this, since <math id="S6.p24.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is
unramified in the special fiber, it must be unramified over <math id="S6.p24.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>;
thus, the fibers are reduced, and the lemma which follows gives flatness,
so we conclude the desired finite étaleness.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p25.1" class="ltx_p">The first statement of the following lemma was provided by Max Lieblich.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.13</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a morphism, with
all fibers of <math id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> reduced. Then if <math id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}:X_{\operatorname{red}}\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}:X_{\operatorname{red}}\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> is flat, we
have that <math id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is reduced. In particular, if <math id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible, <math id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\operatorname{Spec}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\operatorname{Spec}A</annotation></semantics></math> for some DVR <math id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is dominant (still having reduced fibers), then
<math id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is flat.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first assertion follows from Nakayama’s lemma together
with consideration of the exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow{\mathscr{N}}_{X}\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{X}\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{X_{\operatorname{red}}}\rightarrow 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒩</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>red</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow{\mathscr{N}}_{X}\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{X}\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{X_{\operatorname{red}}}\rightarrow 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p26.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p26.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S6.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{N}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒩</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{N}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the sheaf of nilpotents inside <math id="S6.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the second
assertion, we apply the standard criterion for flatness over a DVR twice:
first to <math id="S6.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\operatorname{red}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\operatorname{red}}</annotation></semantics></math> so we can use the lemma to conclude that <math id="S6.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is reduced,
and then again to <math id="S6.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> itself.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 6.14</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that even if <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> maps scheme-theoretically into <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the statement of Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm6" title="Proposition 6.6. ‣ 6. Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a> would be false if we
replaced
<math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1</annotation></semantics></math> in the inequality by <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>: we would expect it to fail precisely at the
intersection of the closed subschemes defined by different choices of
<math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Z}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Z}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{j}+a^{Z}_{r-j}=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{j}+a^{Z}_{r-j}=d</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, it is easy enough to write down examples where one does not have the desired inequality:
the simplest case is <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\cong Z\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≅</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\cong Z\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with affine coordinate
functions <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> vanishing at the node, <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and a
<math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}k[\epsilon]/(\epsilon^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}k[\epsilon]/(\epsilon^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>-valued limit series given by</p>
<table id="S6.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y^{2}+\epsilon y,0),(y+\epsilon,\epsilon),(\epsilon,z+\epsilon)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y^{2}+\epsilon y,0),(y+\epsilon,\epsilon),(\epsilon,z+\epsilon).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here we are identifying sections of <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}(d^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}(d^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> with polynomials of degree <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and for each <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> specifying pairs of sections of degree <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2-i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> on the
components, required to agree at <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=z=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=z=0</annotation></semantics></math>; the inclusion maps are then given
by multiplication by <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> on the appropriate component, and <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> on the
other component. This has <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Z}_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Z}_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math>; note that
modulo <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, it has <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{0}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{0}=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Z}_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Z}_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so it does not correspond
to a refined series, as is required by the proof of Proposition
<a href="#S6.Thmthm7" title="Proposition 6.7. ‣ 6. Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.7</span></a> (iii).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 6.15</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Continuing along the same line of reasoning, we see that the
scheme-theoretic statement of Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm6" title="Proposition 6.6. ‣ 6. Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a> is actually
surprisingly
strong; indeed, it implies that if there are two components of the locus of
refined series which meet at a point of <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, then if the components have
vanishing sequences at the node given by <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{Y}_{j},a^{Z}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{Y}_{j},a^{Z}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime Y}_{j},a^{\prime Z}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime Y}_{j},a^{\prime Z}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we must have <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|a^{Y}_{j}-a^{\prime Y}_{j}|\leq 1,|a^{Z}_{j}-a^{\prime Z}_{j}|\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|a^{Y}_{j}-a^{\prime Y}_{j}|\leq 1,|a^{Z}_{j}-a^{\prime Z}_{j}|\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> for
all <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 6.16</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that it is not hard to deduce from the proof of
Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm6" title="Proposition 6.6. ‣ 6. Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a> than any refined point is the image of an exact
point of the linked
Grassmannian used in the construction (see Definition <a href="#A1.Thmthm10" title="Definition A.10. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.10</span></a>).
However, the converse is false. Indeed, there exist non-refined points for
which there is an exact point above them, and there exist others for which
there isn’t. We see both already in the simplest case of
<math id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\cong Z\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≅</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\cong Z\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2,r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2,r=0</annotation></semantics></math>. In the notation of Remark
<a href="#S6.Thmthm14" title="Remark 6.14. ‣ 6. Comparison to Eisenbud-Harris Theory ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.14</span></a>, we could consider <math id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y^{2},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y^{2},0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y,z)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,z^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,z^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. One
checks that this is not refined, but is an exact point.
On the other hand, if we start with the two
pairs <math id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y^{2},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y^{2},0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,z^{2}+z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,z^{2}+z)</annotation></semantics></math>, it is easy to see that up to scalar the
only way to fill in the middle pair is with <math id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, and this point is not
exact.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Further Questions</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This construction brings up a number of natural further questions, and we
briefly set out a few of them. First, as mentioned earlier, the
Eisenbud-Harris limit series scheme on a reducible curve was never
connected. However, in our case it seems as though the crude limit series
ought to serve as bridges between components of refined limit series with
differing ramification sequences at the node. In fact, at first blush it may
appear based on dimension-counting that crude limit series should simply be
the closure of the refined series in many cases, and this may well be true
in the Eisenbud-Harris scenario of only looking at a <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔤</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{g}}^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> on each
component, but because our crude series will often map with
positive-dimensional fibers to the Eisenbud-Harris crude series, the geometry
is not entirely clear. For similar reasons, even though our construction
<span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a priori</span> gives results on smoothing of crude series, the expected
dimension
hypothesis for all limit series will not follow immediately from having the
expected dimension of refined series. We can
therefore reasonably ask:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ques">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When is the space of limit series on a reducible curve
connected?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ques">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 7.2</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When is the space of refined limit series dense in the space of
all limit series?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ques">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 7.3</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In characteristic <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, what can we say about the dimension of
spaces of crude limit
series, and by extension their smoothability? In particular, can we smooth a
“general” crude series, as we can in the case of refined series (the
latter follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, Thm. 4.5]</cite>)?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We remark that bounding the dimension of crude series on a reducible curve,
given an understanding of dimensions of linear series on each component,
should be a combinatorial problem, and if the bound is restrictive enough to
imply that on a general curve the crude series have dimension at most as
large as the dimension of refined series, it will follow that for a general
reducible curve, we can always apply the strong form (that is, part (ii))
of Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 5.4. ‣ 5. Representability ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> to our entire <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> space. In particular,
we can actually make use of the properness of the constructed <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> scheme
to obtain direct arguments for theorems such as Brill-Noether, without
requiring arguments involving blowing up the family, as used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, p.
261]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given our inability to adequately describe the <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued points of <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math>, we can also ask:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ques">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 7.4</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Does <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math> actually map scheme-theoretically into
<math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EH</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r}_{d,\operatorname{EH}}(X/B)</annotation></semantics></math>? Is it scheme-theoretically surjective?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In applications, an important direction of
generalization is specified ramification along one or more unspecified
smooth sections; this may now be accomplished just as with the case of the
Eisenbud-Harris theory, by looking at positive-dimensional
“special fibers” and allowing <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> to become negative; see
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, p. 270]</cite> for an example.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the transparency of the construction presented here offers various
possibilities for generalization beyond the setting of linear series on
curves of compact type. One direction of generalization is to replace
curves by higher-dimensional varieties. To carry out our main theorem
in this setting seems at this point to be just a formality, but its
application presents considerable challenges, the most formidable of which
is that the “expected dimension”
hypothesis of our main theorem is suddenly more of a burden in dimension
higher than one. This is amply demonstrated by the interpolation problem
(see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>), where one sees first that expected
dimension for general ramification points need not hold, even for
zero-dimensional linear series on <math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and second, that standard
degeneration arguments have thus far failed to succeed in describing when
exactly the expected dimension is in fact correct.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">One could also hope to generalize from line bundles to vector bundles. This
should not be too difficult, but in order to set up inductive degeneration
arguments, one would then need some a description of the limit objects on
the reducible curve
which would play the role of the Eisenbud-Harris description of limit
series. Lastly, it might be possible to adapt the construction to work on
curves not of compact type, but in this setting one may find that the
functor for a given family, after restriction to the reducible special
fiber, will still depend on the geometry of the entire family. This would
potentially complicate the situation considerably.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>The Linked Grassmannian Scheme</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this appendix, we develop of a theory of a moduli scheme parametrizing
collections of sub-bundles of vector bundles on a base scheme, linked
together via maps between the vector bundles. Representability by a proper
scheme is easy and true quite generally; however, to obtain dimension
formulas will require more hypotheses and more work. These
hypotheses, while reasonably natural and easy to state, are motivated by the
idea that the vector bundle maps are induced as pushforwards of certain
maps of sufficiently ample line bundles on a scheme proper over the base
scheme, as in the situation of the limit linear series theory of the present
paper, and its natural generalization to higher-dimensional varieties.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first specify the objects we will study in more detail; for the remainder
of this appendix, we will be in:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_sit">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Situation A.1</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be any base scheme, and
<math id="A1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{1},\dots,{\mathscr{E}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{1},\dots,{\mathscr{E}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> vector
bundles on <math id="A1.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, each of rank <math id="A1.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. We have maps
<math id="A1.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:{\mathscr{E}}_{i}\rightarrow{\mathscr{E}}_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:{\mathscr{E}}_{i}\rightarrow{\mathscr{E}}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}:{\mathscr{E}}_{i+1}\rightarrow{\mathscr{E}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}:{\mathscr{E}}_{i+1}\rightarrow{\mathscr{E}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a positive integer <math id="A1.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The functor we wish to study may now be easily described:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.2</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this situation, we have the functor
<math id="A1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{LG}(r,\{{\mathscr{E}}_{i}\}_{i},\{f_{i},g_{i}\}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{LG}(r,\{{\mathscr{E}}_{i}\}_{i},\{f_{i},g_{i}\}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, associating to each <math id="A1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="A1.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> the set of sub-bundles
<math id="A1.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},\dots,V_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},\dots,V_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{1,T},\dots,{\mathscr{E}}_{n,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{1,T},\dots,{\mathscr{E}}_{n,T}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="A1.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfying
<math id="A1.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i,T}(V_{i})\subset V_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i,T}(V_{i})\subset V_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i,T}(V_{i+1})\subset V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i,T}(V_{i+1})\subset V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Then without any further hypotheses, we have:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.3</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{LG}(r,\{{\mathscr{E}}_{i}\}_{i},\{f_{i},g_{i}\}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{LG}(r,\{{\mathscr{E}}_{i}\}_{i},\{f_{i},g_{i}\}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is representable by
a projective scheme <math id="A1.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="A1.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, which is naturally a closed subscheme of
a product <math id="A1.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> of Grassmannian schemes over <math id="A1.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="A1.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and
projective over <math id="A1.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of relative dimension <math id="A1.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nr(d-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nr(d-r)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the schemes of Grassmannians of rank <math id="A1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> sub-bundles of the
<math id="A1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> the product of the <math id="A1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="A1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote our projection
maps from <math id="A1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to each <math id="A1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the maps from each <math id="A1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
to <math id="A1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{F}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℱ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{F}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal sub-bundles on each <math id="A1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then each <math id="A1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a map</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{i}^{*}{\mathscr{F}}_{i}\rightarrow\pi_{i}^{*}\phi_{i}^{*}{\mathscr{E}}_{i}=\pi_{i+1}^{*}\phi_{i+1}^{*}{\mathscr{E}}_{i}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f_{i}}}{{\rightarrow}}\pi_{i+1}^{*}\phi_{i+1}^{*}{\mathscr{E}}_{i+1}\rightarrow\pi_{i+1}^{*}\phi_{i+1}^{*}{\mathscr{E}}_{i+1}/\pi_{i+1}^{*}{\mathscr{F}}_{i+1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℱ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mover><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i}^{*}{\mathscr{F}}_{i}\rightarrow\pi_{i}^{*}\phi_{i}^{*}{\mathscr{E}}_{i}=\pi_{i+1}^{*}\phi_{i+1}^{*}{\mathscr{E}}_{i}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle f_{i}}}{{\rightarrow}}\pi_{i+1}^{*}\phi_{i+1}^{*}{\mathscr{E}}_{i+1}\rightarrow\pi_{i+1}^{*}\phi_{i+1}^{*}{\mathscr{E}}_{i+1}/\pi_{i+1}^{*}{\mathscr{F}}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">on <math id="A1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the kernel of this map is a closed subscheme which imposes precisely the
condition that <math id="A1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(V_{i})\subset V_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(V_{i})\subset V_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, <math id="A1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a map
<math id="A1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{i+1}^{*}{\mathscr{F}}_{i+1}\rightarrow\pi_{i}^{*}\phi_{i}^{*}{\mathscr{E}}_{i}/\pi_{i}^{*}{\mathscr{F}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℱ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i+1}^{*}{\mathscr{F}}_{i+1}\rightarrow\pi_{i}^{*}\phi_{i}^{*}{\mathscr{E}}_{i}/\pi_{i}^{*}{\mathscr{F}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="A1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> whose kernel imposes the condition <math id="A1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}(V_{i+1})\subset V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}(V_{i+1})\subset V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking
the intersection of these closed subschemes for all <math id="A1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> thus
gives a scheme representing <math id="A1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{LG}(r,\{E_{i}\}_{i},\{f_{i},g_{i}\}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{LG}(r,\{E_{i}\}_{i},\{f_{i},g_{i}\}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, which as a
closed subscheme of <math id="A1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is projective over <math id="A1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">However, in order to say anything of substance about the <math id="A1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LG</annotation></semantics></math> scheme itself,
and in particular to get the necessary lower bound on
dimension, we need to make a number of additional hypotheses. We define:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.4</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Situation <a href="#A1.Thmthm1" title="Situation A.1. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>, we say that
<math id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}(r,\{E_{i}\}_{i},\{f_{i},g_{i}\}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>LG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}(r,\{E_{i}\}_{i},\{f_{i},g_{i}\}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">linked
Grassmannian</span> of <span id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">length <math id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> if <math id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is integral and Cohen-Macaulay, and
the following
additional conditions on the <math id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are satisfied:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(I)</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists some <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in{\mathscr{O}}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in{\mathscr{O}}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}g_{i}=g_{i}f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}g_{i}=g_{i}f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is
scalar multiplication by <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(II)</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Wherever <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, the kernel of <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely equal to
the image of <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and vice versa. More precisely, for any <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and given any
two integers <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}+r_{2}&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}+r_{2}&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>, then the closed
subscheme of <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> obtained as the locus where <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has rank less than or
equal to <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has rank less than or equal to <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is empty.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(III)</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At any point of <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}f_{i}\cap\ker f_{i+1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}f_{i}\cap\ker f_{i+1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}g_{i+1}\cap\ker g_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}g_{i+1}\cap\ker g_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely, for any integer <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and any
<math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we have locally closed subschemes of <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the locus
where <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has rank exactly <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i+1}f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i+1}f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has rank less than or
equal to <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly for the <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we require simply that
all of these subschemes be empty.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark A.5</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The hypothesis that <math id="A1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is integral and Cohen-Macaulay is
unnecessary for most of our analysis. We use it only in the
dimension theory portion of the argument, to ensure that <math id="A1.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> is catenary.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">From this point on, we strengthen Situation <a href="#A1.Thmthm1" title="Situation A.1. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_sit">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Situation A.6</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We suppose that <math id="A1.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> is a linked Grassmannian, and we
denote its structure map to <math id="A1.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A1.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The following lemma will be convenient for constructing and manipulating
points of <math id="A1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.7</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{V_{i}\}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{V_{i}\}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be a k-valued point of <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
suppose <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for any <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we can decompose <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})\oplus\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}\oplus C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})\oplus\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}\oplus C</annotation></semantics></math> for some complementary subspace <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, if we specify any <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}\subset\ker g_{i-1}|_{V_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}\subset\ker g_{i-1}|_{V_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> which intersects
<math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math> trivially, we may choose <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=C^{\prime}\oplus C^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=C^{\prime}\oplus C^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for some
<math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Clearly, it suffices to show that for any <math id="A1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> as in the
statement, we have that <math id="A1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})\oplus\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}\oplus C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})\oplus\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}\oplus C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
injects into <math id="A1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. But suppose we have <math id="A1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}\in f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}\in f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}\in\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}\in\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{3}\in C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{3}\in C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="A1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}+v_{2}+v_{3}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}+v_{2}+v_{3}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. If we apply
<math id="A1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, by hypothesis <math id="A1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i-1}(v_{3})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i-1}(v_{3})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i-1}(v_{1})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i-1}(v_{1})=0</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="A1.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
is in the image of <math id="A1.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and we assumed <math id="A1.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0</annotation></semantics></math>. So we find that
<math id="A1.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i-1}(v_{2})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i-1}(v_{2})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which we claim implies <math id="A1.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>: indeed, <math id="A1.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}\in\ker f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}\in\ker f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
by hypothesis, so by condition (II) of a linked Grassmannian it is in
the image of <math id="A1.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and by condition (III), it cannot map to <math id="A1.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> under
<math id="A1.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> unless it is <math id="A1.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="A1.p10.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="A1.p10.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}+v_{3}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}+v_{3}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and since we
assumed that <math id="A1.p10.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> was disjoint from <math id="A1.p10.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, we get <math id="A1.p10.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}=v_{3}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}=v_{3}=0</annotation></semantics></math>
as well.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">In order to make inductive arguments convenient, we define:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.8</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> is a linked Grassmannian of length <math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> any
positive integer less than <math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the <span id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">truncation map</span> from
<math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> to the linked Grassmannian of length <math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by forgetting all
<math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We will want to know that the truncation map is always surjective, even on
certain classes of families:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.9</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The truncation map is surjective for all <math id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further, in the case that the base is a point, let <math id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\{V_{i}\}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\{V_{i}\}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be any
point of <math id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math>, and suppose we have a family
<math id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{n^{\prime}}=\tilde{V}_{i}|_{i\leq n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{n^{\prime}}=\tilde{V}_{i}|_{i\leq n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> (that is, a scheme-valued point
of the restricted <math id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LG</annotation></semantics></math> scheme) specializing to the truncation of
<math id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to length <math id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and such that <math id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{V}_{n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{V}_{n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> may be written as
<math id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{C}_{n^{\prime}}\oplus\ker f_{n^{\prime}}|_{V_{n^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{C}_{n^{\prime}}\oplus\ker f_{n^{\prime}}|_{V_{n^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some family
<math id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{C}_{n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{C}_{n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> may be lifted to a family <math id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,
specializing to <math id="A1.Thmthm9.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, possibly after a Zariski localization of the base of
the family.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Surjectivity may be checked on points, and given the description
of the <math id="A1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> functor, it suffices to handle the case <math id="A1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Over a point, we may consider <math id="A1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{1}={\mathscr{E}}_{2}=E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{1}={\mathscr{E}}_{2}=E</annotation></semantics></math> to be a single <math id="A1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector space, and <math id="A1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to
be self-maps of <math id="A1.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A1.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a vector space of dimension <math id="A1.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="A1.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>;
we just need to show that there exists a <math id="A1.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="A1.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="A1.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="A1.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(V_{1})\subset V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(V_{1})\subset V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}(V_{2})\subset V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}(V_{2})\subset V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, or
equivalently, such that <math id="A1.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(V_{1})\subset V_{2}\subset g_{1}^{-1}(V_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(V_{1})\subset V_{2}\subset g_{1}^{-1}(V_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now, <math id="A1.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f_{1}(V_{1})\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f_{1}(V_{1})\leq r</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(V_{1})\subset g_{1}^{-1}(V_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(V_{1})\subset g_{1}^{-1}(V_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> by hypothesis, so it suffices to observe
that <math id="A1.p13.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim g_{1}^{-1}(V_{1})=\dim\ker g_{1}+\dim(V_{1}\cap\operatorname{im}g_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim g_{1}^{-1}(V_{1})=\dim\ker g_{1}+\dim(V_{1}\cap\operatorname{im}g_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the codimension of <math id="A1.p13.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}g_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}g_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p13.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="A1.p13.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded by
<math id="A1.p13.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\ker g_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\ker g_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we conclude that <math id="A1.p13.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim g_{1}^{-1}(V_{1})\geq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim g_{1}^{-1}(V_{1})\geq r</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the second assertion, it suffices to show that we can lift to a
<math id="A1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{V}_{n^{\prime}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{V}_{n^{\prime}+1}</annotation></semantics></math> of the form <math id="A1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{C}_{n^{\prime}+1}\oplus\ker f_{n^{\prime}+1}|_{V_{n^{\prime}+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{C}_{n^{\prime}+1}\oplus\ker f_{n^{\prime}+1}|_{V_{n^{\prime}+1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and specializing to the truncation of <math id="A1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to
length <math id="A1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}+1</annotation></semantics></math>, since then we can iterate until we have lifted all the way to
length <math id="A1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Thanks to Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmthm7" title="Lemma A.7. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.7</span></a>, we can write <math id="A1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n^{\prime}}=C_{n^{\prime}}\oplus\ker f_{n^{\prime}}|_{V_{n^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n^{\prime}}=C_{n^{\prime}}\oplus\ker f_{n^{\prime}}|_{V_{n^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n^{\prime}+1}=f_{n^{\prime}}(V_{n^{\prime}})\oplus\ker f_{n^{\prime}+1}|_{V_{n^{\prime}+1}}\oplus C_{n^{\prime}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n^{\prime}+1}=f_{n^{\prime}}(V_{n^{\prime}})\oplus\ker f_{n^{\prime}+1}|_{V_{n^{\prime}+1}}\oplus C_{n^{\prime}+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for some
<math id="A1.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n^{\prime}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n^{\prime}+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A1.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{C}_{n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{C}_{n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> specializing to <math id="A1.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
in particular, having full rank under <math id="A1.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> except possibly on a
closed subset of the base supported away from <math id="A1.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, where the rank could drop.
Away from this locus on the base, if we replace <math id="A1.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n^{\prime}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n^{\prime}+1}</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="A1.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{n^{\prime}}\tilde{V}_{n^{\prime}}\oplus\ker f_{n^{\prime}+1}|_{V_{n^{\prime}+1}}\oplus C_{n^{\prime}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{n^{\prime}}\tilde{V}_{n^{\prime}}\oplus\ker f_{n^{\prime}+1}|_{V_{n^{\prime}+1}}\oplus C_{n^{\prime}+1}</annotation></semantics></math>
(that is, if we set <math id="A1.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{C}_{n+1}=f_{n^{\prime}}\tilde{V}_{n^{\prime}}\oplus C_{n^{\prime}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{C}_{n+1}=f_{n^{\prime}}\tilde{V}_{n^{\prime}}\oplus C_{n^{\prime}+1}</annotation></semantics></math>), noting that <math id="A1.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{n^{\prime}}\tilde{V}_{n^{\prime}}=f_{n^{\prime}}\tilde{C}_{n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{n^{\prime}}\tilde{V}_{n^{\prime}}=f_{n^{\prime}}\tilde{C}_{n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we
obtain a lifting with the desired properties.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">The key notion for getting a handle on the <math id="A1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> scheme is the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.10</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say that a point of a linked Grassmannian
scheme is <span id="A1.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">exact</span> if the corresponding collection of vector spaces <math id="A1.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfy the conditions that <math id="A1.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker g_{i}|_{V_{i+1}}\subset f_{i}(V_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker g_{i}|_{V_{i+1}}\subset f_{i}(V_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A1.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}\subset g_{i}(V_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}\subset g_{i}(V_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">The last part of assertion (ii) of the following lemma is gratuitous, but it
follows immediately from the argument for the rest, and may perhaps
shed some little light on the overall situation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.11</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have the following description of
exact points:</span></p>
<ul id="A1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The exact points form an open subscheme of
</span><math id="A1.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and are naturally described as the complement of the closed subscheme
on which </span><math id="A1.I2.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}f_{i}|_{V_{i}}+\operatorname{rk}g_{i}|_{V_{i+1}}&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}f_{i}|_{V_{i}}+\operatorname{rk}g_{i}|_{V_{i+1}}&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for some </span><math id="A1.I2.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the case </span><math id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, we find that we can describe exact points as
those with
</span><math id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}f_{i}(V_{i})+\operatorname{rk}g_{i}(V_{i+1})=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}f_{i}(V_{i})+\operatorname{rk}g_{i}(V_{i+1})=r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, even for arbitrary
scheme-valued points, and we also find that an exact point has </span><math id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(V_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(V_{i})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> a
sub-bundle of </span><math id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}(V_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}(V_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> a sub-bundle of </span><math id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all
</span><math id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.ix2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">We certainly get a closed subscheme as described, simply by
taking the union over all <math id="A1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},r_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},r_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}+r_{2}&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}+r_{2}&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math> of the loci
described by <math id="A1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}f_{i}|_{V_{i}}\leq r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}f_{i}|_{V_{i}}\leq r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}g_{i}|_{V_{i+1}}\leq r_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}g_{i}|_{V_{i+1}}\leq r_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We
immediately see that the points of this set are precisely the complement of
the exact points, since outside the locus where <math id="A1.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, both <math id="A1.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A1.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are invertible, and correspondingly all points are exact; on the other
hand, if
<math id="A1.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes at our point, we have <math id="A1.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}f_{i}\subset\ker g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}f_{i}\subset\ker g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A1.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}g_{i}\subset\ker f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}g_{i}\subset\ker f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, so we already have that
<math id="A1.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f_{i}(V_{i})+\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f_{i}(V_{i})+\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})\leq r</annotation></semantics></math> and we get strict inequality if
and only if these containments are strict.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the second part, if a <math id="A1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued point
satisfies <math id="A1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}f_{i}|_{V_{i}}+\operatorname{rk}g_{i}|_{V_{i+1}}=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}f_{i}|_{V_{i}}+\operatorname{rk}g_{i}|_{V_{i+1}}=r</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> on all of <math id="A1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>,
by definition it is in the complement of the closed subscheme of non-exact
points defined above, so it is certainly exact. Conversely, for the other
direction it suffices to work over local rings, so suppose we
have a <math id="A1.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued point <math id="A1.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is local, and the point
<math id="A1.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bar{V}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bar{V}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> at the closed point of <math id="A1.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is exact.
Since <math id="A1.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is zero on <math id="A1.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, then for any given <math id="A1.p18.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="A1.p18.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\bar{f}_{i}|_{\bar{V}_{i}}=\bar{g}_{i}(\bar{V}_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\bar{f}_{i}|_{\bar{V}_{i}}=\bar{g}_{i}(\bar{V}_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> and vice versa; in particular, if
we choose
<math id="A1.p18.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{v}_{1},\dots,\bar{v}_{r_{1}}\in\bar{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{v}_{1},\dots,\bar{v}_{r_{1}}\in\bar{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the
<math id="A1.p18.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}_{i}(\bar{v}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}_{i}(\bar{v}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> form a basis of <math id="A1.p18.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}_{i}(\bar{V}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}_{i}(\bar{V}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="A1.p18.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{v}^{\prime}_{1},\dots,\bar{v}^{\prime}_{r_{2}}\in\bar{V}_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{v}^{\prime}_{1},\dots,\bar{v}^{\prime}_{r_{2}}\in\bar{V}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the
<math id="A1.p18.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{g}_{i}(\bar{v}^{\prime}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{g}_{i}(\bar{v}^{\prime}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> form a basis of <math id="A1.p18.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{g}_{i}(\bar{V}_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{g}_{i}(\bar{V}_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, we
find that <math id="A1.p18.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}+r_{2}=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}+r_{2}=r</annotation></semantics></math>, and we obtain a basis <math id="A1.p18.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{e}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{e}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp.,
<math id="A1.p18.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{e}^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{e}^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) for <math id="A1.p18.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp., <math id="A1.p18.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{V}_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{V}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>) given by
the <math id="A1.p18.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{v}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{v}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p18.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}(\bar{v}^{\prime}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}(\bar{v}^{\prime}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> (resp., <math id="A1.p18.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{v}^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{v}^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A1.p18.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(\bar{v}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(\bar{v}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>). By Nakayama’s lemma, we can lift this situation to the
local ring, and easily check that the desired assertions follow.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Our main technical lemma for this appendix is:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.12</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have the following statements on exact
points:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm12.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A1.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The exact points are dense in </span><math id="A1.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I3.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and indeed dense in every
fiber.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given any exact point </span><math id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>LG</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, let </span><math id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be its image in </span><math id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
suppose </span><math id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a local ring, and </span><math id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> a quotient of </span><math id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Let </span><math id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\operatorname{Spec}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\operatorname{Spec}A</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
and </span><math id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}=\operatorname{Spec}A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}=\operatorname{Spec}A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then given any commutative diagram containing the solid
arrows of</span></p>
