<p id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with the closed point of </span><math id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> mapping to </span><math id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the dashed arrow may also be
filled in. In particular, </span><math id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a smooth point of </span><math id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> over </span><math id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I3.ix2.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">For (i),
To see that the exact points are dense in every fiber, suppose we have a
non-exact
point; we just observed that this corresponds to a set of <math id="A1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for
at least one <math id="A1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f_{i}(V_{i})+\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f_{i}(V_{i})+\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, we are in the situation where <math id="A1.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}g_{i}=g_{i}f_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}g_{i}=g_{i}f_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now, choose the
smallest <math id="A1.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f_{i}(V_{i})+\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f_{i}(V_{i})+\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math>, and truncate
our linked Grassmannian to <math id="A1.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1</annotation></semantics></math>;
here, we show that there are nearby points in the fiber such that
the condition <math id="A1.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f_{i}(V_{i})+\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f_{i}(V_{i})+\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})=r</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied. We leave
<math id="A1.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="A1.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> unmodified. By hypothesis, there are vectors in
<math id="A1.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the kernel of <math id="A1.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> which are not in <math id="A1.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(V_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(V_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and
vice versa; indeed, we see that <math id="A1.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}:=\dim\ker g_{i}|_{V_{i+1}}-\dim f_{i}(V_{i})=\dim\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}-\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})=r-\dim f_{i}(V_{i})-\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}:=\dim\ker g_{i}|_{V_{i+1}}-\dim f_{i}(V_{i})=\dim\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}-\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})=r-\dim f_{i}(V_{i})-\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Choose <math id="A1.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p20.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p20.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p20.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker g_{i}|_{V_{i+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker g_{i}|_{V_{i+1}}</annotation></semantics></math> of
dimension <math id="A1.p20.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, intersecting <math id="A1.p20.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}(V_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}(V_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p20.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(V_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(V_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> trivially; we
have that together with these spaces, they must complete
the span of <math id="A1.p20.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p20.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker g_{i}|_{V_{i+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker g_{i}|_{V_{i+1}}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively.
Since <math id="A1.p20.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}\subset\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}\subset\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is in <math id="A1.p20.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we can find
<math id="A1.p20.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\dots,e_{r^{\prime}}\in{\mathscr{E}}_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\dots,e_{r^{\prime}}\in{\mathscr{E}}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose span is necessarily disjoint from
<math id="A1.p20.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and which map to a
basis of <math id="A1.p20.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="A1.p20.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmthm7" title="Lemma A.7. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.7</span></a>, we can write
<math id="A1.p20.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i+1}=f_{i}(V_{i})\oplus\ker f_{i+1}|_{V_{i+1}}\oplus C_{i+1}\oplus C^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i+1}=f_{i}(V_{i})\oplus\ker f_{i+1}|_{V_{i+1}}\oplus C_{i+1}\oplus C^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A1.p20.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. If we take any
basis <math id="A1.p20.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\prime}_{1},\dots,e^{\prime}_{r^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\prime}_{1},\dots,e^{\prime}_{r^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.p20.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can make a family
<math id="A1.p20.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{V}_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{V}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="A1.p20.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing <math id="A1.p20.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> with the span of <math id="A1.p20.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\prime}_{i}+te_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\prime}_{i}+te_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.p20.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="A1.p20.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> varies.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, <math id="A1.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{V}_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{V}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> specializes to
<math id="A1.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="A1.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and we see that it always remains linked to <math id="A1.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},\dots,V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},\dots,V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, left unmodified: it certainly maps into <math id="A1.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="A1.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, since we
are modifying basis elements by the <math id="A1.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which were chosen to map into
<math id="A1.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>; on the other hand, our construction leaves the summand
<math id="A1.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(V_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(V_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> unmodified, so <math id="A1.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> certainly maps <math id="A1.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> into any member of
<math id="A1.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{V}_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{V}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We also observe that we now have <math id="A1.p21.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f_{i}(V_{i})+\dim g_{i}(\tilde{V}_{i+1})=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f_{i}(V_{i})+\dim g_{i}(\tilde{V}_{i+1})=r</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="A1.p21.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>: indeed,
<math id="A1.p21.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> was in the kernel of <math id="A1.p21.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.p21.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so we still have
<math id="A1.p21.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}(\tilde{V}_{i+1})\supset g_{i}(V_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}(\tilde{V}_{i+1})\supset g_{i}(V_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math>; for any
<math id="A1.p21.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p21.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> maps isomorphically to <math id="A1.p21.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="A1.p21.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>; finally,
since we chose <math id="A1.p21.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to, together with <math id="A1.p21.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}(V_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}(V_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, span
<math id="A1.p21.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we find that for any <math id="A1.p21.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p21.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}(\tilde{V}_{i+1})=\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}(\tilde{V}_{i+1})=\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, giving the desired exactness at <math id="A1.p21.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, by Lemma
<a href="#A1.Thmthm9" title="Lemma A.9. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.9</span></a>, we can lift this family to a family <math id="A1.p21.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{V}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{V}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for all
<math id="A1.p21.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, specializing to our given point, but now satisfying
<math id="A1.p21.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f_{i}(V_{i})+\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f_{i}(V_{i})+\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})=r</annotation></semantics></math> for a general point in the family; we
conclude that the points which are non-exact at the <math id="A1.p21.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th step (but exact
for <math id="A1.p21.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math>) are in the closure of those which are exact through the <math id="A1.p21.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th
step, and by induction are actually in the closure of the points which are
exact at all steps.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">For assertion (ii), <math id="A1.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a collection <math id="A1.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{V_{i}\}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{V_{i}\}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="A1.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>;
Our <math id="A1.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are now all free modules of rank <math id="A1.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="A1.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, and we simply want
to produce free <math id="A1.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-submodules <math id="A1.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (with free quotients) linked
by the <math id="A1.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
and restricting to the given <math id="A1.p22.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in the quotient ring <math id="A1.p22.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To do this, denote by <math id="A1.p22.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> the collection of subspaces over
the residue field of <math id="A1.p22.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to <math id="A1.p22.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="A1.p22.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i},r_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i},r_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the
dimensions of <math id="A1.p22.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(\bar{V}_{i}),g_{i}(\bar{V}_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(\bar{V}_{i}),g_{i}(\bar{V}_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> respectively for each <math id="A1.p22.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
We begin by choosing bases <math id="A1.p22.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{e}^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{e}^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p22.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and lifting
appropriately. If our <math id="A1.p22.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p22.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are invertible at the closed point,
which is to say, if the <math id="A1.p22.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> from
condition (I) of a linked Grassmannian is non-zero in <math id="A1.p22.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, we simply
choose an arbitrary basis <math id="A1.p22.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{e}^{1}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{e}^{1}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p22.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{V}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{V}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and take its images
under the <math id="A1.p22.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then lift the <math id="A1.p22.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{e}^{1}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{e}^{1}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p22.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and take images
under the <math id="A1.p22.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, to obtain bases of the <math id="A1.p22.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and lift by the same process
to <math id="A1.p22.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, defining sub-modules <math id="A1.p22.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">Otherwise, if we had <math id="A1.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, for each <math id="A1.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> we choose
<math id="A1.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{e}^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{e}^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in three
categories: first, <math id="A1.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-r_{i-1}-r^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-r_{i-1}-r^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> elements which are linearly independent
from the span of <math id="A1.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}(\bar{V}_{i-1})\cup g_{i}(\bar{V}_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}(\bar{V}_{i-1})\cup g_{i}(\bar{V}_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math>; second,
<math id="A1.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> elements generating <math id="A1.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}(\bar{V}_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}(\bar{V}_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math>; and third,
<math id="A1.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> elements generating <math id="A1.p23.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}(\bar{V}_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}(\bar{V}_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Noting that
even without exactness, since <math id="A1.p23.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A1.p23.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\dim f_{i-1}(\bar{V}_{i-1})+\dim\ker f_{i-1}|_{\bar{V}_{i-1}}\geq r_{i-1}+r^{\prime}_{i-1}\geq r_{i-1}+r^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\dim f_{i-1}(\bar{V}_{i-1})+\dim\ker f_{i-1}|_{\bar{V}_{i-1}}\geq r_{i-1}+r^{\prime}_{i-1}\geq r_{i-1}+r^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="A1.p23.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}(\bar{V}_{i-1})\subset\ker g_{i-1}|_{\bar{V}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}(\bar{V}_{i-1})\subset\ker g_{i-1}|_{\bar{V}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is
disjoint from <math id="A1.p23.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}(\bar{V}_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}(\bar{V}_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math>,
so we see that this is possible. Moreover, by choosing the first category
for all <math id="A1.p23.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> first, we can inductively construct the basis elements in the
second and third categories to be images under <math id="A1.p23.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p23.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of basis
elements already chosen, moving from <math id="A1.p23.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p23.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=n</annotation></semantics></math> for the second category,
and the opposite direction for the third.
Next, choose lifts <math id="A1.p23.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="A1.p23.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, using the same process of lifting
all <math id="A1.p23.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{e}^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{e}^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in the first category first, and defining the rest as
iterated images under <math id="A1.p23.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p23.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, lift the <math id="A1.p23.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="A1.p23.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{e}^{i}_{j}\in{\mathscr{E}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{e}^{i}_{j}\in{\mathscr{E}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, once again via the same process, and define
<math id="A1.p23.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the span of the <math id="A1.p23.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{e}^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{e}^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Nakayama’s lemma, the <math id="A1.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> constructed in either case give free
generators for the <math id="A1.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying Nakayama’s lemma again, we find that
the <math id="A1.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are sub-bundles of <math id="A1.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="A1.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, and clearly they
specialize to the <math id="A1.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we need only check that they are linked. In the
case that <math id="A1.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> was non-zero in <math id="A1.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="A1.p24.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are linked under
the <math id="A1.p24.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by construction, and must likewise be linked under the <math id="A1.p24.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
since <math id="A1.p24.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a unit times the inverse of <math id="A1.p24.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the case where <math id="A1.p24.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> was
zero in <math id="A1.p24.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, take any <math id="A1.p24.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{e}^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{e}^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.p24.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>; we show that its
image under <math id="A1.p24.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a scalar multiple of <math id="A1.p24.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{e}^{i+1}_{j^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{e}^{i+1}_{j^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some
<math id="A1.p24.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. We now apply exactness, to note that either <math id="A1.p24.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(\bar{e}^{i}_{j})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(\bar{e}^{i}_{j})=0</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A1.p24.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{e}^{i}_{j}=g_{i}(\tilde{e}^{i+1}_{j^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{e}^{i}_{j}=g_{i}(\tilde{e}^{i+1}_{j^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>, or we defined
<math id="A1.p24.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{e}^{i+1}_{j^{\prime}}=f_{i}(\tilde{e}^{i}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{e}^{i+1}_{j^{\prime}}=f_{i}(\tilde{e}^{i}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="A1.p24.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the latter
case, we are done, while in the former case we simply observe that
<math id="A1.p24.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(\tilde{e}^{i}_{j})=s(\tilde{e}^{i+1}_{j^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(\tilde{e}^{i}_{j})=s(\tilde{e}^{i+1}_{j^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The same argument
works for the <math id="A1.p24.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we have constructed a map from <math id="A1.p24.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p24.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LG</annotation></semantics></math> lifting
<math id="A1.p24.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, which by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, Prop. 17.14.2]</cite> completes the proof of part (ii).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p25.1" class="ltx_p">The following proposition provides a strong converse to part (ii) of the
above lemma:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition A.13</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The non-exact points of a fiber are
precisely the intersections of the components of that fiber.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since the exact points are smooth, they are certainly not in
any intersection of components. For the other direction, we first make the
following observation: because ranks can only drop under specialization,
given two exact points <math id="A1.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{V_{i}\}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{V_{i}\}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{V^{\prime}_{i}\}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{V^{\prime}_{i}\}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A1.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}:=\dim f_{i}(V_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}:=\dim f_{i}(V_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}_{i}:=\dim f_{i}(V^{\prime}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}_{i}:=\dim f_{i}(V^{\prime}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, if some <math id="A1.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}\neq r^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}\neq r^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the
two points must lie on distinct components of <math id="A1.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, to show that any
non-exact point is in the intersection of components, it suffices to exhibit
it as the specialization of two different exact points with distinct <math id="A1.p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text">Looking at the proof of Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmthm12" title="Lemma A.12. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.12</span></a> part (i), we see that any
point which is non-exact at <math id="A1.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A1.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> minimal, can expressed as the
specialization of an exact point with <math id="A1.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> unchanged for all <math id="A1.p27.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq i_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq i_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>;
however, upon closer examination, we see that in fact the process leaves all the
<math id="A1.p27.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> unchanged, simply increasing the dimensions of the <math id="A1.p27.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}(V_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}(V_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> as
necessary to make the points exact. On the other hand, we note that the
linked Grassmannian situation is completely symmetric in the <math id="A1.p27.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A1.p27.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, so now that we have shown that any point can be written as the
specialization of an exact point with the <math id="A1.p27.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f_{i}(V_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f_{i}(V_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> unchanged, it
follows by symmetry that there is another exact point specializing to our
given point, leaving the dimensions of the <math id="A1.p27.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}(V_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}(V_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> intact,
and therefore necessarily increasing at least some of the <math id="A1.p27.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. This then
expresses our non-exact point as lying in the intersection of two
components, as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p28.1" class="ltx_p">We can also use the smoothness at exact points to compute the dimension of
fibers of <math id="A1.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.14</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The fibers of <math id="A1.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="A1.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> have every
component of dimension precisely <math id="A1.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(d-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(d-r)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text">In view of Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmthm12" title="Lemma A.12. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.12</span></a>,
we can compute the dimension of any component of the fiber
by showing that its tangent space at any exact point has the desired
dimension. Since we are only looking at a fiber, we set <math id="A1.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\operatorname{Spec}k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\operatorname{Spec}k</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A1.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}\cong{\mathbb{G}}(r,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>LG</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}\cong{\mathbb{G}}(r,d)</annotation></semantics></math>, and is smooth of dimension <math id="A1.p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(d-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(d-r)</annotation></semantics></math>, so
there is nothing to show. Otherwise, suppose we have a collection of <math id="A1.p29.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
corresponding to an exact point. Then <math id="A1.p29.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}=g_{i}(V_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker f_{i}|_{V_{i}}=g_{i}(V_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> for all
<math id="A1.p29.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, so we use Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmthm7" title="Lemma A.7. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.7</span></a> to write each <math id="A1.p29.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as
<math id="A1.p29.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})\oplus g_{i}(V_{i+1})\oplus C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})\oplus g_{i}(V_{i+1})\oplus C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for some complementary
space <math id="A1.p29.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our first assertion is that the dimensions <math id="A1.p29.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="A1.p29.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
add up to <math id="A1.p29.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, if we let <math id="A1.p29.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}=\dim f_{i}(V_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}=\dim f_{i}(V_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="A1.p29.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}_{i}=\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}_{i}=\dim g_{i}(V_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A1.p29.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}=r-r^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}=r-r^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from exactness, and for
<math id="A1.p29.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;i&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;i&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p29.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}=r-r_{i-1}-r^{\prime}_{i}=r_{i}-r_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}=r-r_{i-1}-r^{\prime}_{i}=r_{i}-r_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with
<math id="A1.p29.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}=r-r^{\prime}_{1}=r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}=r-r^{\prime}_{1}=r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p29.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n}=r-r_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n}=r-r_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we see we indeed have <math id="A1.p29.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}d_{i}=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}d_{i}=r</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text">The next claim is that first-order deformations of the <math id="A1.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> inside of <math id="A1.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math>
correspond precisely to first-order deformations of each <math id="A1.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> individually
inside <math id="A1.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, taken modulo deformations of the <math id="A1.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> which remain inside
<math id="A1.p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Any deformation of the <math id="A1.p30.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> together will yield a deformation of
the <math id="A1.p30.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>: we use our direct sum decomposition to inductively define the
induced deformation, obtaining deformations of <math id="A1.p30.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(V_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(V_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> as the image of
the deformation of <math id="A1.p30.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> together with the (inductively obtained)
deformation of <math id="A1.p30.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly for the <math id="A1.p30.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}(V_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}(V_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, since each <math id="A1.p30.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(V_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(V_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by <math id="A1.p30.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}(V_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math> together
with <math id="A1.p30.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, this is the only possible way to obtain a deformation of the
<math id="A1.p30.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> given deformations of the <math id="A1.p30.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Clearly, two deformations of the <math id="A1.p30.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> will yield equivalent deformations of
<math id="A1.p30.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if their difference is a deformation of the <math id="A1.p30.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> inside
of its <math id="A1.p30.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, any deformation of the <math id="A1.p30.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> may be expressed
(non-uniquely) as a deformation of its summands, and in particular gives
a deformation of the <math id="A1.p30.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, at least up to the same equivalence relation.
Since the deformation of the <math id="A1.p30.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the deformations of the <math id="A1.p30.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
was unique, this must invert our first construction, completing the proof
of the claim.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we are done: first-order deformations of any given <math id="A1.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are given by
the tangent space to <math id="A1.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{G}}(d_{i},d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{G}}(d_{i},d)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a variety smooth of dimension
<math id="A1.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}(d-d_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}(d-d_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, so has <math id="A1.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}(d-d_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}(d-d_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional tangent space at any point.
Similarly, the
space of deformations of <math id="A1.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> inside of <math id="A1.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension <math id="A1.p31.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}(r-d_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}(r-d_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>;
the
difference is <math id="A1.p31.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}(d-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}(d-r)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the total dimension of our tangent space is
<math id="A1.p31.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}d_{i}(d-r)=r(d-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}d_{i}(d-r)=r(d-r)</annotation></semantics></math>, as asserted.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p32.1" class="ltx_p">We now have all the tools to prove our main result:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A.15</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmthm15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A linked Grassmannian scheme is a closed subscheme
of the obvious product of Grassmannian schemes over <math id="A1.Thmthm15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>; it is
projective over <math id="A1.Thmthm15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and each component has codimension <math id="A1.Thmthm15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)r(d-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)r(d-r)</annotation></semantics></math>
inside the product, and maps surjectively to <math id="A1.Thmthm15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A1.Thmthm15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>
is non-zero, then <math id="A1.Thmthm15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> is also irreducible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text">We already have that the linked Grassmannian is projective over
<math id="A1.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and lies inside the obvious product of Grassmannians, which we denote
by <math id="A1.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easy to see
each component maps dominantly onto <math id="A1.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, since the exact points are both
smooth and dense by Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmthm12" title="Lemma A.12. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.12</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p34.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the dimension statement,
given any component of <math id="A1.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A1.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> be an exact point of <math id="A1.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> on the
specified component, and not on any other component, and <math id="A1.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> the image of <math id="A1.p34.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="A1.p34.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A1.p34.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Cohen-Macaulay, everything is catenary, so codimensions
can be computed naively for irreducible spaces. By Lemma
<a href="#A1.Thmthm14" title="Lemma A.14. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.14</span></a>, we have that
<math id="A1.p34.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{\operatorname{LG},x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>LG</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{\operatorname{LG},x}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth over <math id="A1.p34.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{S,s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{S,s}</annotation></semantics></math> of relative dimension <math id="A1.p34.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(d-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(d-r)</annotation></semantics></math>, and
in particular integral. Similarly, <math id="A1.p34.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{G,x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{G,x}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally affine over
<math id="A1.p34.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{S,s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{S,s}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence integral and smooth of relative dimension <math id="A1.p34.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nr(d-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nr(d-r)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The desired codimension statement then follows from
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, Prop. 17.5.8 (i)]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p35.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, when <math id="A1.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is non-zero, over the open subset of <math id="A1.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p35.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is
invertible, the fibers are all simply Grassmannians of dimension <math id="A1.p35.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(d-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(d-r)</annotation></semantics></math>;
since the map is proper, we conclude that <math id="A1.p35.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible over
this locus, of dimension <math id="A1.p35.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(d-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(d-r)</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, since every component
maps dominantly to <math id="A1.p35.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, there cannot be any component of <math id="A1.p35.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> contained in
the locus where <math id="A1.p35.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, yielding the desired irreducibility.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_warn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Warning A.16</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmthm11" title="Lemma A.11. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.11</span></a> sounds quite innocuous, but there
are some
pitfalls to be aware of. Consider the simple example of <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=d=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\operatorname{Spec}k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\operatorname{Spec}k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{1}={\mathscr{E}}_{2}=k^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{1}={\mathscr{E}}_{2}=k^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}=\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;0\\
0&amp;1\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}=\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;0\\
0&amp;1\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this case, if <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by
<math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}X_{0}\\
X_{1}\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}X_{0}\\
X_{1}\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}=\begin{bmatrix}Y_{0}\\
Y_{1}\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}=\begin{bmatrix}Y_{0}\\
Y_{1}\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math>, we find the condition for them
to be linked is simply that <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}Y_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}Y_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is easy enough to check that
we actually get that <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> is scheme-theoretically cut out by this equation
inside <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{1}\times{\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{1}\times{\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, giving a pair of <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s attached at <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}=Y_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}=Y_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the only non-exact point. Our lemma has shown that deformations
have to behave well at the exact points, but if we consider the <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued
point for <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\operatorname{Spec}k[\epsilon]/(\epsilon^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\operatorname{Spec}k[\epsilon]/(\epsilon^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by
<math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}\epsilon\\
1\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>ϵ</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}\epsilon\\
1\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by
<math id="A1.Thmthm16.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\
\epsilon\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>ϵ</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\
\epsilon\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math>, we note two pathologies:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm16.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm16.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, this point actually satisfies our initial set-theoretic description
of an exact point, that <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker g_{1}|_{V_{2}}\subset f_{1}(V_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker g_{1}|_{V_{2}}\subset f_{1}(V_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and vice versa, as
both images and kernels will be given precisely by <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. So
this description, while dealing with both <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> invertible
simultaneously, is only valid from a set-theoretic point of view.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm16.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm16.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, while we have shown that at any (scheme-valued) exact point, there
will be an <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}+r_{2}=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}+r_{2}=r</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}f_{1}|_{V_{1}}\leq r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}f_{1}|_{V_{1}}\leq r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A1.Thmthm16.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}g_{1}|_{V_{2}}\leq r_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}g_{1}|_{V_{2}}\leq r_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that by allowing the ranks to drop at the
closed point, we actually allow them to increase on the local ring level.
Specifically, in our case <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so either <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> would have to be
<math id="A1.Thmthm16.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, but neither <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> nor <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the zero map. Of course, this makes
perfect sense geometrically, as the node will necessarily have tangent
vectors which don’t point along either branch, but it underscores the fact
that the <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued points of a union of schemes is not simply the union of
the <math id="A1.Thmthm16.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-valued points of the individual schemes.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p36.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude with an example and some further questions which we have not
pursued here because they are not necessary for our applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example A.17</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm17.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the situation of <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\operatorname{Spec}k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\operatorname{Spec}k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case,
it is easy to describe the components explicitly, as well as to see their
dimensions without invoking any deformation theory. We already know that if
<math id="A1.Thmthm17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we just get a Grassmannian, so we assume that <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0</annotation></semantics></math>. If we write
<math id="A1.Thmthm17.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}=\operatorname{rk}f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}=\operatorname{rk}f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}=\operatorname{rk}g_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}=\operatorname{rk}g_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (on the entire vector space), we have <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}+d_{2}=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}+d_{2}=d</annotation></semantics></math> by condition (II) of a linked Grassmannian. We will see that
there are <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\{r+1,d-r+1,d_{1}+1,d_{2}+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\{r+1,d-r+1,d_{1}+1,d_{2}+1\}</annotation></semantics></math> components, each of dimension
<math id="A1.Thmthm17.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(d-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(d-r)</annotation></semantics></math>, and indexed by the dimension of <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(V_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(V_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> on general points.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm17.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, we saw in the proof of Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmthm9" title="Lemma A.9. ‣ Appendix A The Linked Grassmannian Scheme ‣ A Limit Linear Series Moduli Scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.9</span></a> that
the fiber of any point <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> under truncation is simply the
Grassmannian of vector spaces <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(V_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(V_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and contained in
<math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}^{-1}(V_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}^{-1}(V_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, which had dimension
<math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\ker g_{1}+\dim(V_{1}\cap\operatorname{im}g_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\ker g_{1}+\dim(V_{1}\cap\operatorname{im}g_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We need to see that this dimension depends only on the dimension of
<math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(V_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(V_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, which we
will denote by <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. By condition (II) of a linked
Grassmannian, <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker g_{1}=\operatorname{im}f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker g_{1}=\operatorname{im}f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}g_{1}=\ker f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}g_{1}=\ker f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we may
write this as <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}+\dim(V_{1}\cap\ker f_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}+\dim(V_{1}\cap\ker f_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(V_{1}\cap\ker f_{1})=r-r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(V_{1}\cap\ker f_{1})=r-r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we can write everything in terms of <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, as
desired. Specifically, we have a Grassmannian of <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspaces of
a <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{1}+r-r_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{1}+r-r_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space, containing an <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space,
and this has dimension <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r-r_{1})(d_{1}-r_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r-r_{1})(d_{1}-r_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm17.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We now obtain our assertions without trouble: fix an
<math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}\leq\min\{r,d_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}\leq\min\{r,d_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> also satisfying <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}\geq\max\{0,r-d_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}\geq\max\{0,r-d_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
consider the locally closed subset <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r_{1}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r_{1}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f_{1}(V_{1})=r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f_{1}(V_{1})=r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the specified range is precisely the range
for which this will be non-empty. Now, <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r_{1}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r_{1}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open subset of the
locus in <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim f_{1}(V_{1})\leq r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim f_{1}(V_{1})\leq r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which corresponds simply to a
Schubert cycle, which is irreducible of codimension <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r-r_{1})(d_{1}-r_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r-r_{1})(d_{1}-r_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. If
we base change <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r_{1}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r_{1}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get a proper map with irreducible equidimensional fibers,
mapping surjectively to an irreducible base, so in fact <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes
irreducible, and has dimension precisely <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(d-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(d-r)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since this dimension
remains constant
as <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases, and the codimension of <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{r_{1}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{r_{1}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> increases as <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
decreases, we find we must have exactly one irreducible component of <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math>
for each choice of <math id="A1.Thmthm17.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ques">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question A.18</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Can we show that <math id="A1.Thmthm18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> is flat over <math id="A1.Thmthm18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>? That it is reduced?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ques">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question A.19</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Can we describe the components of <math id="A1.Thmthm19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LG}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.Thmthm19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
